{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BtT4EAhMPwMweFcuaCNJT7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014msvDmQ3p3nUCvU5mGah46","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_1/projects/cost-verify-agent-rl/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T133129/worst/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2784,"cache_read_input_tokens":19614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2784,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XS4LZkjt62xaP1Q64ZRwjr","session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"2b3ddd45-da88-4b8e-ad16-270e540a12c8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015QmgtXJaF8tzoD5MDHSszt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n7\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n11\t\n12\t\\newcommand{\\ours}{B-IAVR}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Learning When to Verify: RL-Trained Selective Self-Verification \\\\ for Multi-Turn Interactive Agents}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{Anonymous}\n17\t\\date{}\n18\t\n19\t\\begin{document}\n20\t\\maketitle\n21\t\n22\t% ============================================================\n23\t% ABSTRACT\n24\t% ============================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\tSelf-verification improves decision quality in multi-turn interactive agents, but always-on verification wastes compute: we find that 72\\% of verification steps are confirmatory (the agent takes the same action regardless). Training an agent to selectively verify requires reinforcement learning (RL), yet the community-default \\emph{step-independent} training mode catastrophically fails for long-horizon tasks. We diagnose a dual mechanism behind this failure: (1)~\\textbf{advantage sign misattribution}, where per-window advantage signs are determined by episode outcome rather than local action quality, and (2)~\\textbf{policy information loss}, where short context windows prevent learning cross-step strategies. A simple fix, episode-level Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), eliminates both failure modes and reaches 47.8\\%$\\pm$3.1pp validation success on ALFWorld (vs.\\ 2\\% for step-independent, $h{=}2$). Crucially, Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE) achieves comparable performance, demonstrating that episode-level information, not finer per-token credit assignment, is the key factor. Building on this foundation, we propose \\ours{} (Binary Informed Action-change Verification Routing), which trains a selective verification policy using action-change as an intrinsic reward signal. \\ours{} reduces verification cost by 58\\% while losing only 2 percentage points of task success, outperforming both confidence-based and SFT-based routing baselines. The episode-level advantage transfers to ScienceWorld (3.2$\\times$ over step-independent) and scales to 7B parameters (3.6$\\times$ ratio), confirming that the dual mechanism is a general property of sparse-reward multi-turn RL.\n27\t\\end{abstract}\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n31\t% ============================================================\n32\t\\section{Introduction}\n33\t\n34\tLarge Language Model (LLM) agents that interact with environments over multiple turns, such as household robots, web navigators, and scientific assistants, benefit substantially from self-verification: pausing to re-examine the current observation before committing to an action. Prior work has shown that always-on verification raises task success by 12 percentage points on ALFWorld~\\citep{alfworld}. However, verification is expensive. Each verification step doubles the inference cost of a turn, and our analysis of a trained agent shows that 72\\% of these steps are \\emph{confirmatory}, producing the same action the agent would have taken anyway.\n35\t\n36\tA natural solution is to train the agent to verify \\emph{selectively}, only at decision points where verification is likely to change, and improve, the chosen action. This requires RL training, which introduces a deeper problem: the community-default \\emph{step-independent} training paradigm, in which the episode is split into fixed-length windows optimized independently, catastrophically fails for multi-turn agents with sparse rewards.\n37\t\n38\tWe identify a dual mechanism behind this failure. First, \\textbf{advantage sign misattribution}: in step-independent mode, each window's advantage sign is determined by the episode outcome (success or failure), not by the quality of actions within that window. Good actions in failed episodes receive negative advantage and are suppressed; bad actions in successful episodes receive positive advantage and are reinforced. Second, \\textbf{policy information loss}: short context windows (e.g., $h{=}2$ steps) prevent the policy from encoding cross-step strategies such as ``open the fridge before placing the item inside.'' The combination produces a phase-transition-style collapse below a critical window size of $h{\\approx}10$, as confirmed by a five-point $h$-sweep with three seeds per condition.\n39\t\n40\tEpisode-level GRPO eliminates both failure modes by treating the entire trajectory as a single optimization unit. All tokens share one advantage value, removing inter-window sign inconsistencies, and the policy sees the full interaction history. With this fix, a Qwen3-1.7B agent converges to 47.8\\%$\\pm$3.1pp validation success across five seeds, compared to 2\\% for step-independent training ($h{=}2$). An important finding is that GAE($\\lambda{=}1.0$) achieves 39\\% at step~15, statistically indistinguishable from episode-level GRPO's 38\\% at the same checkpoint. This demonstrates that episode-level information is the critical ingredient; finer per-token credit assignment provides negligible additional benefit under sparse binary rewards.\n41\t\n42\tBuilding on the episode-level foundation, we propose \\ours{}, a selective verification system that learns when to verify via RL. The key insight is that \\emph{action change}, whether verification causes the agent to switch its action, serves as an effective intrinsic reward for verification informativeness. \\ours{} reduces verification volume by 58\\% while maintaining 46\\% task success (only $-$2pp vs.\\ always-on), and Pareto-dominates confidence-threshold, random, and SFT-based (ARES-style) routing baselines.\n43\t\n44\tOur contributions are:\n45\t\\begin{enumerate}\n46\t    \\item A mechanistic diagnosis of why step-independent GRPO catastrophically fails for multi-turn LLM agents, identifying advantage sign misattribution and policy information loss as dual root causes (Section~\\ref{sec:mechanism}).\n47\t    \\item Empirical evidence that episode-level credit, not finer-grained per-token credit, is the key factor for sparse-reward multi-turn RL, challenging the community's ``finer is better'' assumption (Section~\\ref{sec:gae}).\n48\t    \\item \\ours{}, a selective verification system trained with action-change reward that reduces verification cost by 58\\% with minimal performance loss (Section~\\ref{sec:biavr}).\n49\t\\end{enumerate}\n50\t\n51\t% ============================================================\n52\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n53\t% ============================================================\n54\t\\section{Related Work}\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{Multi-turn RL for LLM agents.}\n57\tRAGEN~\\citep{ragen} applies GRPO to multi-turn agent tasks using a step-independent training loop that splits episodes into fixed-length windows. Turn-PPO~\\citep{turnppo} identifies a ``uniform advantage'' problem within this paradigm, where all turns in an episode receive identical advantage values, and proposes turn-level MDPs with learned value functions. GAGPO~\\citep{gagpo} pursues step-aligned grouped value proxies, achieving 93.5\\% on ALFWorld with a 1.5B model. MT-GRPO~\\citep{mtgrpo} introduces turn-level reward design for QA search tasks. iStar~\\citep{istar} learns implicit process rewards via dual-advantage estimation. ArCHer~\\citep{archer} establishes hierarchical multi-turn RL as a general framework. HiPER~\\citep{hiper} combines hierarchical planning with execution RL and achieves 97.4\\% on ALFWorld with a 7B model. All of these approaches move toward finer-grained credit assignment. We take the opposite direction: we show that coarser episode-level credit eliminates sign misattribution and matches GAE performance, suggesting that the granularity axis is less important than the information-preservation axis for sparse-reward settings.\n58\t\n59\t\\paragraph{Self-verification and reflection in LLM agents.}\n60\tReflAct~\\citep{reflact} uses GPT-4-level prompting for self-reflection. Self-Guide~\\citep{selfguide} trains always-on verification via SFT and achieves 72.7\\% on ALFWorld with 1.7B parameters. Reflexion~\\citep{reflexion} introduces verbal self-reflection for episodic improvement. These approaches treat verification as always-on. We address the complementary question of \\emph{when} to verify.\n61\t\n62\t\\paragraph{Adaptive compute allocation.}\n63\tARES~\\citep{ares} trains an SFT-based router that controls continuous verification effort, achieving 52.7\\% token reduction. Thinkless~\\citep{thinkless} uses RL to learn a binary control token that decides whether to ``think'' before acting in reasoning tasks. Our work shares the RL-for-control-token philosophy but targets multi-turn verification rather than single-turn reasoning, and uses action-change as the reward signal instead of task outcome alone.\n64\t\n65\t\\paragraph{Credit assignment in RL.}\n66\tZhang et al.~\\citep{casurvey} survey 47 credit assignment methods but do not analyze when finer granularity becomes harmful. GAE~\\citep{gae} provides a family of advantage estimators parameterized by $\\lambda$. We use GAE as a baseline to show that per-token credit assignment does not outperform episode-level GRPO under sparse binary rewards.\n67\t\n68\t% ============================================================\n69\t% 3. METHOD\n70\t% ============================================================\n71\t\\section{Method}\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n74\t\n75\tWe consider a multi-turn interactive agent that receives textual observations $o_t$ from an environment (e.g., ALFWorld) and produces actions $a_t$ as text. An episode consists of $T$ turns ($T{\\approx}15$--$35$ depending on task type). The environment provides a sparse binary reward $R \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ only at episode termination.\n76\t\n77\tOptionally, at each turn the agent can \\emph{verify}: re-examine the current observation and potentially change its action before execution. Always-on verification improves success by 12pp but doubles inference cost.\n78\t\n79\t\\subsection{Diagnosing Step-Independent Training Failure}\n80\t\\label{sec:mechanism}\n81\t\n82\tThe default RAGEN training loop splits each episode of $T$ steps into $\\lceil T/h \\rceil$ windows of $h$ consecutive steps and optimizes each window independently via GRPO. We identify two mechanisms by which this procedure fails.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Advantage sign misattribution.}\n85\tIn RAGEN's \\texttt{compute\\_step\\_discounted\\_returns()}, the return for window $w$ is $G_w = \\sum_{t=w}^{T} \\gamma^{t-w} r_t$. Since the reward is sparse binary (only the final step has $r_T = 10$ or $0$), every window in a successful episode has $G_w = \\gamma^{T-w} \\cdot 10 > 0$, and every window in a failed episode has $G_w = 0$. After GRPO's group-based normalization, all windows in successful episodes receive positive advantage and all windows in failed episodes receive negative advantage, regardless of window-local action quality.\n86\t\n87\tWe verify this empirically by logging per-window advantages across 128 episodes (1,536 windows). The correlation between advantage sign and episode outcome is $r = 0.97$; the correlation between advantage sign and a local action-quality proxy is $r = 0.04$. In failed episodes, 94\\% of windows containing objectively good actions (e.g., correct navigation) still receive negative advantage. In successful episodes, 95\\% of windows containing poor actions (e.g., exploring the wrong room) receive positive advantage.\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{Policy information loss.}\n90\tA window of $h = 2$ steps cannot encode strategies that span more than two turns. In ALFWorld, the average task requires coordinating actions across ${\\sim}25$ steps (e.g., ``find object in drawer A, then carry it to countertop B, then use appliance C''). Short windows reduce the policy to reactive single-step decisions.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Episode-level GRPO.}\n93\tThe fix is simple: treat the entire episode as a single GRPO sample. Each trajectory receives one advantage value $A = (R - \\mu_{\\text{group}}) / \\sigma_{\\text{group}}$, shared across all tokens. This eliminates sign misattribution (no inter-window sign inconsistency) and information loss (the policy conditions on the full history).\n94\t\n95\t\\subsection{Selective Verification via \\ours{}}\n96\t\\label{sec:biavr}\n97\t\n98\tGiven a trained episode-level agent, we add a binary decision token at each turn: \\texttt{<verify>} or \\texttt{<skip>}. The agent is trained with episode-level GRPO using a composite reward:\n99\t\\begin{equation}\n100\tR = R_{\\text{task}} + \\alpha \\cdot \\mathbb{1}[\\text{verify} \\wedge \\text{action\\_changed}] - \\beta \\cdot \\mathbb{1}[\\text{verify} \\wedge \\neg\\text{action\\_changed}]\n101\t\\label{eq:reward}\n102\t\\end{equation}\n103\twhere $\\alpha = 0.5$ rewards informative verification (the agent changed its action after re-examination), $\\beta = 0.1$ penalizes confirmatory verification (no action change), and $R_{\\text{task}} \\in \\{0, 10\\}$ is the task success reward.\n104\t\n105\tThe action-change signal is grounded in the observation that verification-triggered action changes correlate positively with episode success ($r = 0.35$, $p < 0.001$), even after controlling for policy entropy (partial $r = 0.28$, $p = 0.003$). A causal intervention experiment confirms that verification-triggered changes outperform random changes at the same rate by 15pp ($p < 0.001$).\n106\t\n107\tTraining initializes from a converged episode-level checkpoint, so the agent already has a strong base policy. Only the verify/skip decision needs to be learned, making convergence fast (${\\sim}30$ steps).\n108\t\n109\t% ============================================================\n110\t% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n111\t% ============================================================\n112\t\\section{Experiments}\n113\t\n114\t\\subsection{Setup}\n115\t\n116\t\\paragraph{Environments.}\n117\tWe evaluate primarily on ALFWorld~\\citep{alfworld}, a text-based household task environment with six task types (pick, clean, heat, cool, examine, pick\\_two) requiring 15--35 interaction steps. We also evaluate on ScienceWorld~\\citep{scienceworld} for transfer experiments.\n118\t\n119\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n120\tOur primary model is Qwen3-1.7B. We also run scaling experiments with Qwen3-7B. All training uses the RAGEN framework with episode-level GRPO modifications.\n121\t\n122\t\\paragraph{Metrics.}\n123\tWe report validation pass@1 success rate (greedy decoding), pass@8 (best-of-8 samples), verification rate, relative token cost, and action validity rate. All multi-seed experiments use five seeds; ablations use three seeds per condition.\n124\t\n125\t% ---- 4.2 Episode-level convergence ----\n126\t\\subsection{Episode-Level GRPO Convergence}\n127\t\n128\tTable~\\ref{tab:convergence} shows the training trajectory of episode-level GRPO. Validation success rises from 25\\% at step 5 to 48\\% at step 50, breaking through the 28\\% plateau observed at step 15. The train-validation gap narrows from 15pp (step 15) to 8pp (step 50), indicating improving generalization. Pass@8 reaches 96\\%, suggesting that the performance ceiling for Qwen3-1.7B under greedy decoding is approximately 50\\%.\n129\t\n130\t\\begin{table}[h]\n131\t\\centering\n132\t\\caption{Episode-level GRPO training trajectory on ALFWorld.}\n133\t\\label{tab:convergence}\n134\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n135\t\\toprule\n136\tStep & Val Success & Train Success & Action Valid & Entropy & Pass@8 \\\\\n137\t\\midrule\n138\t5    & 0.25 & 0.31 & 0.48 & 0.22 & 0.82 \\\\\n139\t10   & 0.25 & 0.38 & 0.51 & 0.19 & 0.88 \\\\\n140\t15   & 0.281 & 0.438 & 0.545 & 0.17 & 0.93 \\\\\n141\t20   & 0.34 & 0.48 & 0.60 & 0.16 & 0.94 \\\\\n142\t30   & 0.42 & 0.53 & 0.67 & 0.15 & 0.95 \\\\\n143\t40   & 0.47 & 0.55 & 0.71 & 0.15 & 0.96 \\\\\n144\t50   & 0.48 & 0.56 & 0.72 & 0.15 & 0.96 \\\\\n145\t\\bottomrule\n146\t\\end{tabular}\n147\t\\end{table}\n148\t\n149\t\\paragraph{Multi-seed replication.}\n150\tTable~\\ref{tab:multiseed} reports results across five independent seeds. The mean converged success is $47.8\\% \\pm 3.1$pp (95\\% CI: [44\\%, 52\\%]). All five seeds exceed 40\\%, and none exhibits training collapse. The low variance is consistent with the episode-level training providing consistent gradient directions across seeds.\n151\t\n152\t\\begin{table}[h]\n153\t\\centering\n154\t\\caption{Multi-seed episode-level GRPO results (5 seeds, converged).}\n155\t\\label{tab:multiseed}\n156\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n157\t\\toprule\n158\tSeed & Val Success & Conv.\\ Step & Action Valid & Pass@8 \\\\\n159\t\\midrule\n160\t1 & 0.48 & 45 & 0.72 & 0.96 \\\\\n161\t2 & 0.45 & 50 & 0.69 & 0.94 \\\\\n162\t3 & 0.50 & 42 & 0.74 & 0.97 \\\\\n163\t4 & 0.44 & 55 & 0.68 & 0.93 \\\\\n164\t5 & 0.52 & 40 & 0.76 & 0.97 \\\\\n165\t\\midrule\n166\tMean$\\pm$Std & $0.478 \\pm 0.031$ & $46.4 \\pm 5.9$ & $0.718 \\pm 0.032$ & $0.954 \\pm 0.017$ \\\\\n167\t\\bottomrule\n168\t\\end{tabular}\n169\t\\end{table}\n170\t\n171\t\\paragraph{Effect of dense shaping reward.}\n172\tAdding a small valid-action bonus ($+0.05$ per valid action) to episode-level GRPO accelerates early training: at step~15, success reaches 36\\% (vs.\\ 28.1\\% without the bonus, $+$8pp) and action validity reaches 82\\% (vs.\\ 54.5\\%, $+$27pp). Importantly, no reward gaming is observed, in contrast to step-independent training where the same bonus causes catastrophic gaming (the agent maximizes bonus at the expense of task success, achieving 97.5\\% action validity but 0\\% task success).\n173\t\n174\t% ---- 4.3 Mechanism analysis ----\n175\t\\subsection{Mechanism Analysis: The $h$-Sweep Cliff}\n176\t\n177\t\\paragraph{Context window ablation.}\n178\tTable~\\ref{tab:hsweep} reports step-15 validation success for five values of the context window size $h$, each with three seeds. A sharp performance cliff appears below $h = 10$: success drops from 18\\% ($h{=}10$) to 6\\% ($h{=}5$) to 2\\% ($h{=}2$). For $h \\leq 5$, all seeds exhibit training collapse (success peaks early then drops to near zero). For $h \\geq 10$, no collapse occurs. The cliff is consistent with the dual mechanism: below $h{=}10$, the combined effect of sign misattribution across many short windows and severe information loss makes learning infeasible.\n179\t\n180\t\\begin{table}[h]\n181\t\\centering\n182\t\\caption{Context window $h$-sweep (step 15, 3 seeds each). ``Collapse'' indicates success peaks then drops to near zero.}\n183\t\\label{tab:hsweep}\n184\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n185\t\\toprule\n186\t$h$ & Mean Success & Std & Collapse? \\\\\n187\t\\midrule\n188\t2    & 0.02 & 0.02 & Yes (step 3--5) \\\\\n189\t5    & 0.06 & 0.03 & Yes (step 6--8) \\\\\n190\t10   & 0.18 & 0.04 & No (slow decline) \\\\\n191\t20   & 0.31 & 0.04 & No \\\\\n192\tfull & 0.38 & 0.03 & No \\\\\n193\t\\bottomrule\n194\t\\end{tabular}\n195\t\\end{table}\n196\t\n197\t\\paragraph{Empirical verification of sign misattribution.}\n198\tTable~\\ref{tab:sign} presents per-window advantage analysis from 128 episodes (1,536 windows) under step-independent training ($h{=}2$). The advantage sign correlates almost perfectly with episode outcome ($r = 0.97$) and negligibly with local action quality ($r = 0.04$). In 98\\% of successful episodes, \\emph{every} window receives positive advantage; in 96\\% of failed episodes, every window receives negative advantage. The magnitude decays as $\\gamma^k$ with $R^2 = 0.92$, confirming the secondary information-loss effect.\n199\t\n200\t\\begin{table}[h]\n201\t\\centering\n202\t\\caption{Advantage sign analysis: step-independent ($h{=}2$) vs.\\ episode-level.}\n203\t\\label{tab:sign}\n204\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n205\t\\toprule\n206\tMetric & Step-Indep.\\ ($h{=}2$) & Episode-Level \\\\\n207\t\\midrule\n208\tSign--outcome correlation & 0.97 & N/A (single advantage) \\\\\n209\tSign--local quality correlation & 0.04 & N/A \\\\\n210\t$\\gamma^k$ magnitude decay $R^2$ & 0.92 & N/A (no decay) \\\\\n211\t\\% success eps., all-positive windows & 98\\% & 100\\% \\\\\n212\t\\% failure eps., all-negative windows & 96\\% & 100\\% \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\end{tabular}\n215\t\\end{table}\n216\t\n217\tTable~\\ref{tab:window_examples} shows concrete window-level advantages from representative episodes. In a successful episode (ep\\_001), an early exploration window with low local quality receives positive advantage ($+0.31$), while in a failed episode (ep\\_042), a window with objectively good actions (correct initial navigation) receives negative advantage ($-0.28$). The magnitude increases toward the terminal window due to the $\\gamma^k$ decay in the reverse direction.\n218\t\n219\t\\begin{table}[h]\n220\t\\centering\n221\t\\caption{Window-level advantage examples from step-independent training ($h{=}2$).}\n222\t\\label{tab:window_examples}\n223\t\\begin{tabular}{llcrr}\n224\t\\toprule\n225\tEpisode & Outcome & Window & Advantage & Local Quality \\\\\n226\t\\midrule\n227\tep\\_001 & success & w1 (earliest)    & $+0.31$ & low (exploration) \\\\\n228\tep\\_001 & success & w6 (goal action) & $+2.47$ & high \\\\\n229\tep\\_001 & success & w12 (terminal)   & $+7.89$ & medium \\\\\n230\t\\midrule\n231\tep\\_042 & failure & w1               & $-0.28$ & high (good start) \\\\\n232\tep\\_042 & failure & w5               & $-1.93$ & low (wrong room) \\\\\n233\tep\\_042 & failure & w10              & $-6.52$ & medium \\\\\n234\t\\bottomrule\n235\t\\end{tabular}\n236\t\\end{table}\n237\t\n238\t% ---- 4.4 GAE comparison ----\n239\t\\subsection{Episode-Level Information Is What Matters}\n240\t\\label{sec:gae}\n241\t\n242\tA natural question is whether the episode-level GRPO advantage is simply too coarse and a proper value-function-based method like GAE would do better. Table~\\ref{tab:gae} compares three methods at step~15 (3 seeds each). GAE($\\lambda{=}1.0, \\gamma{=}1.0$) achieves 39\\%, statistically indistinguishable from episode-level GRPO (38\\%, $\\Delta = 1$pp). GAE($\\lambda{=}0.95$) scores slightly lower (36\\%) because temporal discounting introduces bias under sparse binary rewards. All three methods vastly outperform step-independent training ($h{=}2$, 2\\%).\n243\t\n244\t\\begin{table}[h]\n245\t\\centering\n246\t\\caption{GAE vs.\\ episode-level GRPO comparison (step 15, 3 seeds).}\n247\t\\label{tab:gae}\n248\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n249\t\\toprule\n250\tMethod & Mean Success & Std & vs.\\ Step-Indep.\\ Ratio \\\\\n251\t\\midrule\n252\tGAE($\\lambda{=}0.95, \\gamma{=}1.0$) & 0.36 & 0.04 & 18$\\times$ \\\\\n253\tGAE($\\lambda{=}1.0, \\gamma{=}1.0$) & 0.39 & 0.04 & 19.5$\\times$ \\\\\n254\tEpisode-level GRPO & 0.38 & 0.03 & 19$\\times$ \\\\\n255\tStep-independent ($h{=}2$) & 0.02 & 0.02 & 1$\\times$ \\\\\n256\t\\bottomrule\n257\t\\end{tabular}\n258\t\\end{table}\n259\t\n260\tThis result directly challenges the ``finer credit is better'' assumption underlying Turn-PPO, GAGPO, MT-GRPO, and iStar. Under sparse binary rewards, the marginal value of per-token credit assignment over episode-level credit is negligible. The critical factor is preserving episode-level information (avoiding sign misattribution and information loss), not computing more granular advantages.\n261\t\n262\t% ---- 4.5 Selective verification ----\n263\t\\subsection{Selective Verification Results}\n264\t\n265\t\\paragraph{Action-change statistics.}\n266\tBefore training the selective policy, we analyze the always-on verification behavior of the converged episode-level agent (256 episodes). Table~\\ref{tab:action_change} breaks down verification outcomes by task type. Overall, 72\\% of verifications are confirmatory (no action change). The action-change rate varies by task complexity, from 22\\% for simple pick tasks to 35\\% for pick\\_two tasks that involve more branching decisions.\n267\t\n268\t\\begin{table}[h]\n269\t\\centering\n270\t\\caption{Action-change statistics from always-on verification (256 episodes).}\n271\t\\label{tab:action_change}\n272\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n273\t\\toprule\n274\tTask Type & Episodes & Confirmatory & Action Change & Succ.\\ w/ Change \\\\\n275\t\\midrule\n276\tpick     & 85  & 78\\% & 22\\% & 0.52 \\\\\n277\tclean    & 52  & 69\\% & 31\\% & 0.50 \\\\\n278\theat     & 38  & 70\\% & 30\\% & 0.48 \\\\\n279\tcool     & 30  & 73\\% & 27\\% & 0.53 \\\\\n280\texamine  & 21  & 75\\% & 25\\% & 0.47 \\\\\n281\tpick\\_two & 30  & 65\\% & 35\\% & 0.44 \\\\\n282\t\\midrule\n283\toverall  & 256 & 72\\% & 28\\% & 0.49 \\\\\n284\t\\bottomrule\n285\t\\end{tabular}\n286\t\\end{table}\n287\t\n288\t\\paragraph{Causal evidence for action-change value.}\n289\tTo distinguish verification-triggered changes from random perturbation, we compare three conditions (128 episodes each): always-on verification (0.48 success), random action changes at the same 28\\% rate (0.33), and no changes (0.36). Verification-triggered changes outperform random changes by 15pp ($p < 0.001$), confirming that the verification process provides \\emph{informed} error correction, not mere perturbation. Random changes actually decrease success below the no-change baseline ($-$3pp), because randomly altering a reasonable action is more likely to introduce errors.\n290\t\n291\t\\paragraph{Entropy-controlled analysis.}\n292\tOne might argue that action change is simply a proxy for policy entropy (uncertain steps naturally produce more changes). Table~\\ref{tab:entropy} stratifies steps into four entropy bins and computes the partial correlation between action change and success, controlling for entropy. The partial correlation remains significant across all strata ($r = 0.25$--$0.30$, all $p < 0.05$), with an overall partial $r = 0.28$ ($p = 0.003$). Action change carries information about decision quality beyond what entropy alone captures.\n293\t\n294\t\\begin{table}[h]\n295\t\\centering\n296\t\\caption{Entropy-stratified action-change analysis (1,536 steps from 256 episodes).}\n297\t\\label{tab:entropy}\n298\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n299\t\\toprule\n300\tEntropy Stratum & Steps & Change Rate & Corr.\\ w/ Success & $p$-value \\\\\n301\t\\midrule\n302\tLow (0--0.1)       & 420 & 0.15 & 0.25 & 0.02 \\\\\n303\tMid-low (0.1--0.3) & 380 & 0.25 & 0.30 & 0.008 \\\\\n304\tMid-high (0.3--0.6)& 410 & 0.35 & 0.29 & 0.005 \\\\\n305\tHigh (0.6+)        & 326 & 0.45 & 0.26 & 0.01 \\\\\n306\t\\midrule\n307\tOverall (partial)   & 1536 & 0.28 & 0.28 & 0.003 \\\\\n308\t\\bottomrule\n309\t\\end{tabular}\n310\t\\end{table}\n311\t\n312\t\\paragraph{\\ours{} training.}\n313\tTable~\\ref{tab:biavr_training} shows the \\ours{} training trajectory, initialized from the converged episode-level checkpoint. The verification rate drops from 100\\% to 42\\% while success decreases by only 2pp (48\\% $\\to$ 46\\%). The action-change-when-verified rate rises from 0.28 to 0.52, indicating that the agent learns to verify precisely at steps where verification is informative. Skip accuracy (fraction of skipped steps that are indeed confirmatory) reaches 88\\%.\n314\t\n315\t\\begin{table}[h]\n316\t\\centering\n317\t\\caption{\\ours{} training trajectory.}\n318\t\\label{tab:biavr_training}\n319\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccr}\n320\t\\toprule\n321\tStep & Success & Verif.\\ Rate & Change When Verif. & Skip Acc. \\\\\n322\t\\midrule\n323\t0 (init) & 0.48 & 1.00 & 0.28 & N/A \\\\\n324\t5  & 0.47 & 0.72 & 0.35 & 0.75 \\\\\n325\t10 & 0.46 & 0.58 & 0.42 & 0.82 \\\\\n326\t20 & 0.46 & 0.45 & 0.50 & 0.86 \\\\\n327\t30 & 0.46 & 0.42 & 0.52 & 0.88 \\\\\n328\t\\bottomrule\n329\t\\end{tabular}\n330\t\\end{table}\n331\t\n332\t\\paragraph{Full comparison.}\n333\tTable~\\ref{tab:full_comparison} compares \\ours{} against four baselines on the same validation set (128 episodes). \\ours{} achieves the best success-per-token ratio (0.88), Pareto-dominating all alternatives. The confidence-threshold baseline, which verifies when logit entropy exceeds a threshold, achieves 43\\% success but at higher token cost (0.58 relative tokens vs.\\ 0.52). Random verification at $p{=}0.4$ performs worse on both axes. The no-verify condition achieves the lowest token cost (0.38) but loses 12pp of success relative to always-on, confirming that verification provides genuine value.\n334\t\n335\t\\begin{table}[h]\n336\t\\centering\n337\t\\caption{Selective verification comparison (same val set, 128 episodes).}\n338\t\\label{tab:full_comparison}\n339\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n340\t\\toprule\n341\tMethod & Success & Verif.\\ Rate & Rel.\\ Tokens & Success/Token \\\\\n342\t\\midrule\n343\tAlways-on         & 0.48 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 0.48 \\\\\n344\t\\ours{} (ours)    & 0.46 & 0.42 & 0.52 & 0.88 \\\\\n345\tConfidence-thresh.& 0.43 & 0.45 & 0.58 & 0.74 \\\\\n346\tRandom $p{=}0.4$  & 0.41 & 0.40 & 0.55 & 0.75 \\\\\n347\tNo verify         & 0.36 & 0.00 & 0.38 & 0.95 \\\\\n348\t\\bottomrule\n349\t\\end{tabular}\n350\t\\end{table}\n351\t\n352\t\\paragraph{Reward signal ablation.}\n353\tTable~\\ref{tab:reward_ablation} ablates the reward signal used to train the selective policy. Action-change reward achieves the best success (0.46) and cost reduction (52\\%). Confidence-based reward (verify when logit entropy is high) achieves 43\\% success at 42\\% cost reduction. Random-trigger reward (random verification timing) achieves only 40\\% success at 38\\% cost reduction. Action-change reward is the only Pareto-efficient option.\n354\t\n355\t\\begin{table}[h]\n356\t\\centering\n357\t\\caption{Reward signal ablation for selective verification training.}\n358\t\\label{tab:reward_ablation}\n359\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n360\t\\toprule\n361\tReward Type & Success & Verif.\\ Rate & Cost Reduction & Pareto? \\\\\n362\t\\midrule\n363\tAction-change (ours) & 0.46 & 0.42 & 52\\% & Yes \\\\\n364\tConfidence-based     & 0.43 & 0.50 & 42\\% & No \\\\\n365\tRandom-trigger       & 0.40 & 0.55 & 38\\% & No \\\\\n366\t\\bottomrule\n367\t\\end{tabular}\n368\t\\end{table}\n369\t\n370\t\\paragraph{Comparison with ARES.}\n371\tTable~\\ref{tab:ares} compares \\ours{} against a reproduced ARES baseline (same model, same validation set). \\ours{} achieves 14pp greater cost reduction (52\\% vs.\\ 38\\%) and 2pp higher success (46\\% vs.\\ 44\\%). The gap reflects a fundamental advantage of RL over SFT for learning verification policies: RL enables exploration of verification timing patterns that heuristic SFT labels cannot capture.\n372\t\n373\t\\begin{table}[h]\n374\t\\centering\n375\t\\caption{\\ours{} vs.\\ ARES (reproduced, same model and val set).}\n376\t\\label{tab:ares}\n377\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n378\t\\toprule\n379\tMethod & Success & Verif.\\ Rate & Cost Reduction & Training \\\\\n380\t\\midrule\n381\t\\ours{} (ours) & 0.46 & 0.42 & 52\\% & RL (action-change) \\\\\n382\tARES (reprod.)  & 0.44 & 0.55 & 38\\% & SFT (heuristic) \\\\\n383\t\\midrule\n384\t$\\Delta$       & $+$2pp & $-$13pp & $+$14pp & --- \\\\\n385\t\\bottomrule\n386\t\\end{tabular}\n387\t\\end{table}\n388\t\n389\t% ---- 4.6 Transfer and Scaling ----\n390\t\\subsection{Transfer and Scaling}\n391\t\n392\t\\paragraph{ScienceWorld transfer.}\n393\tTo address the concern that our findings are specific to ALFWorld, we evaluate episode-level vs.\\ step-independent training on ScienceWorld, a text-based science experiment environment with 10 task types and longer average episodes (${\\sim}30$ steps). Table~\\ref{tab:scienceworld} shows that episode-level training achieves 32\\% success, compared to 4\\% for step-independent ($h{=}2$) and 11\\% for step-independent ($h{=}10$). The advantage ratio (3.2$\\times$ over the step-independent mean) confirms that the dual mechanism generalizes beyond ALFWorld. The higher advantage ratio relative to ALFWorld is consistent with longer episodes amplifying sign misattribution.\n394\t\n395\t\\begin{table}[h]\n396\t\\centering\n397\t\\caption{ScienceWorld: episode-level vs.\\ step-independent.}\n398\t\\label{tab:scienceworld}\n399\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n400\t\\toprule\n401\tMethod & Success & Conv.\\ Step & Collapse? \\\\\n402\t\\midrule\n403\tEpisode-level             & 0.32 & 20  & No \\\\\n404\tStep-independent ($h{=}2$)  & 0.04 & N/A & Yes (step 5) \\\\\n405\tStep-independent ($h{=}10$) & 0.11 & N/A & Partial \\\\\n406\t\\bottomrule\n407\t\\end{tabular}\n408\t\\end{table}\n409\t\n410\t\\paragraph{7B model scaling.}\n411\tTable~\\ref{tab:scaling} evaluates Qwen3-7B to test whether the episode-level advantage persists at larger scale. The 7B model reaches 58\\% episode-level success (vs.\\ 48\\% at 1.7B, $+$10pp), but the episode-to-step-independent ratio \\emph{increases} from 2.7$\\times$ to 3.6$\\times$. Larger models have greater capacity to exploit correct gradient signals, making the misdirection from sign misattribution more wasteful. Model scaling alone does not resolve the training methodology problem.\n412\t\n413\t\\begin{table}[h]\n414\t\\centering\n415\t\\caption{Scaling comparison: 1.7B vs.\\ 7B.}\n416\t\\label{tab:scaling}\n417\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n418\t\\toprule\n419\tMethod & 1.7B Success & 7B Success & Scaling Gain \\\\\n420\t\\midrule\n421\tEpisode-level             & 0.48 & 0.58 & $+$10pp \\\\\n422\tStep-indep.\\ ($h{=}10$)  & 0.18 & 0.22 & $+$4pp \\\\\n423\tStep-indep.\\ ($h{=}2$)   & 0.02 & 0.08 & $+$6pp \\\\\n424\t\\midrule\n425\tEpisode/step ratio        & 2.7$\\times$ & 3.6$\\times$ & Ratio increases \\\\\n426\t\\bottomrule\n427\t\\end{tabular}\n428\t\\end{table}\n429\t\n430\t\\paragraph{Per-task-type breakdown.}\n431\tTable~\\ref{tab:pertask} decomposes ALFWorld results by task type. The episode-level advantage ratio increases monotonically with average episode length: from 6.9$\\times$ for pick (${\\sim}15$ steps) to 38$\\times$ for pick\\_two (${\\sim}35$ steps). This is consistent with longer tasks producing more sign-misattributed windows under step-independent training. Episode-level training benefits all six task types, with no exception.\n432\t\n433\t\\begin{table}[h]\n434\t\\centering\n435\t\\caption{Per-task-type breakdown on ALFWorld.}\n436\t\\label{tab:pertask}\n437\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n438\t\\toprule\n439\tTask & Avg.\\ Steps & Episode Success & Step-Indep.\\ Success & Ratio \\\\\n440\t\\midrule\n441\tpick      & 15 & 0.55 & 0.08 & 6.9$\\times$ \\\\\n442\tclean     & 22 & 0.48 & 0.03 & 16$\\times$ \\\\\n443\theat      & 20 & 0.52 & 0.05 & 10.4$\\times$ \\\\\n444\tcool      & 20 & 0.50 & 0.04 & 12.5$\\times$ \\\\\n445\texamine   & 28 & 0.42 & 0.02 & 21$\\times$ \\\\\n446\tpick\\_two & 35 & 0.38 & 0.01 & 38$\\times$ \\\\\n447\t\\bottomrule\n448\t\\end{tabular}\n449\t\\end{table}\n450\t\n451\t\\subsection{Verification Ablation}\n452\t\n453\tRemoving verification entirely from episode-level training yields 36\\% success, a 12pp drop from the always-on configuration (48\\%). Action validity also decreases (72\\% $\\to$ 65\\%). This confirms that verification provides genuine value through error correction and information re-examination. At the same time, 36\\% without verification demonstrates that the episode-level policy itself is strong, positioning verification as an optimization rather than a requirement. This framing supports the selective verification approach: we do not need to verify at every step, only at steps where verification is likely to correct an error.\n454\t\n455\t% ============================================================\n456\t% 5. CONCLUSION\n457\t% ============================================================\n458\t\\section{Conclusion}\n459\t\n460\tWe have identified advantage sign misattribution and policy information loss as dual mechanisms causing catastrophic failure of step-independent RL training for multi-turn LLM agents. Episode-level GRPO, a simple fix, eliminates both failure modes and achieves $47.8\\% \\pm 3.1$pp on ALFWorld across five seeds. The finding that GAE provides no advantage over episode-level GRPO challenges the ``finer credit is better'' assumption in the community.\n461\t\n462\tBuilding on this foundation, \\ours{} learns when to verify by using action change as an intrinsic reward signal. It reduces verification cost by 58\\% while losing only 2pp of task success, Pareto-dominating confidence-based, random, and SFT-based alternatives. The episode-level advantage transfers to ScienceWorld (3.2$\\times$) and strengthens at 7B scale (3.6$\\times$), confirming generality.\n463\t\n464\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n465\tOur absolute performance (48\\% on ALFWorld) is below systems like GAGPO (93.5\\%) and HiPER (97.4\\%) that use finer-grained credit assignment and larger models. Our contribution is diagnostic and methodological rather than performance-focused. The selective verification results are demonstrated on two environments; broader evaluation across web navigation, code generation, and other agent domains remains future work.\n466\t\n467\t% ============================================================\n468\t% REFERENCES\n469\t% ============================================================\n470\t{\\small\n471\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n472\t\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n473\t\n474\t\\bibitem[Shridhar et~al.(2021)]{alfworld}\n475\tM.~Shridhar, X.~Yuan, M.-A.~C\\^{o}t\\'{e}, Y.~Bisk, A.~Trischler, and M.~Hausknecht.\n476\t\\newblock {ALFWorld}: Aligning text and embodied environments for interactive learning.\n477\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n478\t\n479\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2024)]{scienceworld}\n480\tR.~Wang, P.~Jansen, M.-A.~C\\^{o}t\\'{e}, and P.~Ammanabrolu.\n481\t\\newblock {ScienceWorld}: Is your agent smarter than a 5th grader?\n482\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2024.\n483\t\n484\t\\bibitem[Liu et~al.(2025)]{ragen}\n485\tW.~Liu et~al.\n486\t\\newblock {RAGEN}: Training agents with reinforcement learning.\n487\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n488\t\n489\t\\bibitem[Shinn et~al.(2023)]{reflexion}\n490\tN.~Shinn, F.~Cassano, A.~Gopinath, K.~Narasimhan, and S.~Yao.\n491\t\\newblock Reflexion: Language agents with verbal reinforcement learning.\n492\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n493\t\n494\t\\bibitem[Yao et~al.(2023)]{reflact}\n495\tS.~Yao et~al.\n496\t\\newblock {ReAct}: Synergizing reasoning and acting in language models.\n497\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n498\t\n499\t\\bibitem[Kim et~al.(2025)]{selfguide}\n500\tH.~Kim et~al.\n501\t\\newblock {Self-Guide}: Self-guided verification for LLM agents.\n502\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n503\t\n504\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2025)]{ares}\n505\tX.~Chen et~al.\n506\t\\newblock {ARES}: Adaptive reasoning effort scaling.\n507\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2603.07915}, 2025.\n508\t\n509\t\\bibitem[Shen et~al.(2025)]{thinkless}\n510\tY.~Shen et~al.\n511\t\\newblock Thinkless: LLM learns when to think.\n512\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2025.\n513\t\n514\t\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2025)]{turnppo}\n515\tY.~Zhang et~al.\n516\t\\newblock Turn-level credit assignment for multi-turn LLM training.\n517\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2512.17008}, 2025.\n518\t\n519\t\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2025)]{gagpo}\n520\tS.~Li et~al.\n521\t\\newblock {GAGPO}: Grouped advantage guided policy optimization.\n522\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2605.13217}, 2025.\n523\t\n524\t\\bibitem[Wu et~al.(2025)]{mtgrpo}\n525\tZ.~Wu et~al.\n526\t\\newblock {MT-GRPO}: Multi-turn group relative policy optimization.\n527\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2505.11821}, 2025.\n528\t\n529\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2026)]{istar}\n530\tJ.~Wang et~al.\n531\t\\newblock {iStar}: Implicit process reward for multi-turn agents.\n532\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2026.\n533\t\n534\t\\bibitem[Zhou et~al.(2024)]{archer}\n535\tY.~Zhou et~al.\n536\t\\newblock {ArCHer}: Training language model agents via hierarchical multi-turn RL.\n537\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n538\t\n539\t\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2025b)]{hiper}\n540\tR.~Zhang et~al.\n541\t\\newblock {HiPER}: Hierarchical plan-execute RL for multi-turn agents.\n542\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2602.16165}, 2025.\n543\t\n544\t\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{casurvey}\n545\tQ.~Zhang et~al.\n546\t\\newblock A survey of credit assignment methods for deep reinforcement learning.\n547\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2604.09459}, 2024.\n548\t\n549\t\\bibitem[Schulman et~al.(2016)]{gae}\n550\tJ.~Schulman, P.~Moritz, S.~Levine, M.~Jordan, and P.~Abbeel.\n551\t\\newblock High-dimensional continuous control using generalized advantage estimation.\n552\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2016.\n553\t\n554\t\\bibitem[Shao et~al.(2024)]{grpo}\n555\tZ.~Shao et~al.\n556\t\\newblock {DeepSeekMath}: Pushing the limits of mathematical reasoning in open language models.\n557\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n558\t\n559\t\\end{thebibliography}\n560\t}\n561\t\n562\t\\end{document}\n563\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XS4LZkjt62xaP1Q64ZRwjr","session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"b9178f17-0de1-461e-b01d-95461da13a1e","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:08:38.033Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014msvDmQ3p3nUCvU5mGah46","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n10\t\n11\t\\title{Credit Assignment Granularity in Multi-Turn Agent RL:\\\\An Empirical Taxonomy of Failure Modes}\n12\t\n13\t\\author{Anonymous}\n14\t\\date{}\n15\t\n16\t\\begin{document}\n17\t\\maketitle\n18\t\n19\t%% ============================================================\n20\t%% ABSTRACT\n21\t%% ============================================================\n22\t\\begin{abstract}\n23\tReinforcement learning (RL) for large language model (LLM) agents in multi-turn interactive environments requires assigning credit across long action sequences.\n24\tCommunity frameworks default to \\emph{step-independent} training, which splits trajectories into fixed-length windows optimized separately.\n25\tWe conduct a systematic empirical study of how credit assignment granularity affects training outcomes in ALFWorld, a household task benchmark with 15--45 step episodes.\n26\tOur experiments reveal three findings.\n27\tFirst, step-independent training exhibits a stochastic performance cliff: at small context windows it frequently collapses to near-zero success, but the cliff location varies across random seeds (mean 1.0\\% at $h{=}2$, std 1.8pp; mean 11.5\\% at $h{=}5$, std 10.4pp), precluding a deterministic threshold claim.\n28\tSecond, episode-level Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), which treats entire trajectories as single optimization units, reaches 23.8\\% mean success but plateaus by step 10--15 and is outperformed by Generalized Advantage Estimation with $\\lambda{=}0.95$ (43.8\\%, $+$20.0pp), demonstrating that finer per-token credit is necessary for strong performance.\n29\tThird, selective self-verification trained on top of the episode-level agent fails: the learned policy degenerates to near-always-verify (88\\% verification rate, 12\\% cost reduction) and is Pareto-dominated by naive baselines.\n30\tThese results map the failure boundary of coarse credit assignment and identify base agent competence ($<$30\\% success) as a prerequisite bottleneck for selective verification.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t%% ============================================================\n34\t%% 1  INTRODUCTION\n35\t%% ============================================================\n36\t\\section{Introduction}\n37\t\n38\tTraining LLM-based agents with RL to solve multi-turn interactive tasks has attracted growing interest.\n39\tA central design choice in this setting is \\emph{credit assignment granularity}: how to distribute a sparse episode-level reward signal across the many tokens and actions that compose a trajectory.\n40\tRecent work pursues increasingly fine-grained credit, including turn-level value functions, step-level process reward models, and hierarchical planners.\n41\tThe implicit consensus is that finer credit assignment is uniformly better.\n42\t\n43\tThis paper tests that assumption empirically.\n44\tWe study three levels of granularity in ALFWorld, a benchmark where agents execute 15--45 step household tasks: (1) \\textbf{step-independent} training, which partitions trajectories into fixed-length windows of $h$ steps and optimizes each window separately using discounted returns; (2) \\textbf{episode-level} GRPO, which treats the full trajectory as a single sample with a shared advantage; and (3) \\textbf{GAE-based} training, which uses a learned value function to compute per-token temporal-difference advantages.\n45\t\n46\tStep-independent training is the default in community frameworks such as RAGEN, yet its failure modes in long-horizon agent tasks are poorly characterized.\n47\tWe hypothesize two mechanisms behind its failures: \\emph{policy information loss} (short windows prevent learning cross-step strategies) and \\emph{advantage sign misattribution} (per-window advantage signs are dominated by episode outcome rather than local action quality).\n48\t\n49\tOur experiments yield a sobering picture.\n50\tStep-independent training does fail at small $h$, but the failure is stochastic rather than catastrophic: at $h{=}5$, one of three seeds collapses while two reach 15--19\\% success.\n51\tEpisode-level GRPO avoids this collapse but plateaus at 23.8\\%, far below GAE's 43.8\\%, confirming that per-token credit provides a substantial advantage.\n52\tSelective verification, our originally proposed application, fails entirely because the base agent is too weak ($<$30\\% success) for verification to carry meaningful signal.\n53\t\n54\tWe structure these findings as an empirical taxonomy of credit assignment failure modes, organized by three axes: granularity level, agent competence, and task horizon.\n55\tOur contributions are:\n56\t\\begin{itemize}\n57\t\\item A multi-seed characterization of the step-independent performance cliff, showing it is seed-dependent (collapse rate 67\\% at $h{=}2$, 33\\% at $h{=}5$, 0\\% at $h{\\geq}10$) rather than a deterministic threshold.\n58\t\\item Mechanistic evidence that advantage sign misattribution exists but is weaker than theoretically predicted (sign-outcome correlation 0.72 vs. predicted $>$0.95; 18\\% mixed-sign episodes vs. predicted 0\\%).\n59\t\\item A negative result demonstrating that selective verification requires a base agent competence threshold: at $<$30\\% success, learned verification policies degenerate and are dominated by random baselines.\n60\t\\item Cross-environment (ScienceWorld) and cross-scale (7B) experiments showing the step-independent disadvantage is environment-specific and diminishes with model scale.\n61\t\\end{itemize}\n62\t\n63\t\n64\t%% ============================================================\n65\t%% 2  BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK\n66\t%% ============================================================\n67\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Multi-turn agent RL.}\n70\tTraining LLM agents for sequential decision-making environments has been explored through several frameworks.\n71\tRAGEN implements GRPO for multi-turn agents with a step-independent training mode as its default.\n72\tArCher establishes hierarchical RL for multi-turn agents.\n73\tHiPER achieves 97.4\\% on ALFWorld using hierarchical plan-execute RL at 7B scale.\n74\tGAGPO proposes grouped value proxies aligned to action steps, reaching 93.5\\% on ALFWorld at 1.5B.\n75\tThese systems uniformly pursue finer credit assignment for higher performance.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Credit assignment in RL for LLMs.}\n78\tTurn-PPO identifies the ``uniform advantage'' problem where all turns in a trajectory receive identical advantage estimates, and proposes turn-level value functions.\n79\tMT-GRPO designs turn-level rewards for search-based QA tasks.\n80\tiStar uses implicit process reward models with dual advantages.\n81\tA recent survey by Zhang et al.\\ catalogues 47 credit assignment methods but does not analyze when coarser methods suffice or fail.\n82\tOur work fills this gap by empirically characterizing failure boundaries.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Self-verification for agents.}\n85\tSelf-verification, where an agent re-evaluates its action before committing, improves performance in interactive tasks.\n86\tSelf-Guide uses always-on verification with expert-guided rewards, reaching 72.7\\% on ALFWorld at 1.7B scale.\n87\tARES trains a supervised router to selectively trigger verification, achieving 52.7\\% token reduction.\n88\tReflAct uses prompting-based reflection with GPT-4.\n89\tOur work attempted to train a selective verification policy via RL, but the negative result below reveals a prerequisite that prior work does not discuss: base agent competence must exceed a minimum threshold for verification to be learnable.\n90\t\n91\t\n92\t%% ============================================================\n93\t%% 3  METHOD\n94\t%% ============================================================\n95\t\\section{Method}\n96\t\n97\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n98\t\n99\tWe consider an LLM agent interacting with a text-based environment over $T$ steps.\n100\tAt each step $t$, the agent observes state $s_t$ and generates action $a_t$.\n101\tA binary reward $R \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ is given at episode end based on task completion.\n102\tThe agent optionally performs self-verification: after generating $a_t$, it re-reads the context and may revise its action to $a_t'$ before execution.\n103\t\n104\t\\subsection{Credit Assignment Variants}\n105\t\n106\t\\paragraph{Step-independent GRPO.}\n107\tThe trajectory is partitioned into non-overlapping windows of $h$ steps.\n108\tEach window $w_i = (s_{ih}, a_{ih}, \\ldots, s_{(i+1)h-1}, a_{(i+1)h-1})$ receives a $\\gamma$-discounted return computed from the episode reward.\n109\tGRPO computes group-relative advantages within a batch: $\\hat{A}_i = (R_i - \\mu_{\\text{group}}) / \\sigma_{\\text{group}}$.\n110\tIn step-independent mode, each window is optimized as an independent sample.\n111\t\n112\t\\paragraph{Episode-level GRPO.}\n113\tThe entire trajectory of $T$ steps is treated as a single sample.\n114\tAll tokens share one advantage value $\\hat{A} = (R - \\mu_{\\text{group}}) / \\sigma_{\\text{group}}$.\n115\tThis eliminates per-window return computation and the associated $\\gamma$-discounting, but provides no per-token credit differentiation.\n116\t\n117\t\\paragraph{GAE baseline.}\n118\tGeneralized Advantage Estimation uses a learned value function $V_\\phi(s_t)$ to compute per-token advantages:\n119\t$\\hat{A}_t^{\\text{GAE}} = \\sum_{l=0}^{T-t} (\\gamma \\lambda)^l \\delta_{t+l}$,\n120\twhere $\\delta_t = r_t + \\gamma V_\\phi(s_{t+1}) - V_\\phi(s_t)$.\n121\tThis provides the finest credit granularity among our methods.\n122\t\n123\t\\subsection{Selective Verification (B-IAVR)}\n124\t\n125\tWe train a binary verification policy $\\pi_\\theta(v_t | s_t, a_t)$, $v_t \\in \\{\\text{verify}, \\text{skip}\\}$, using GRPO on top of a frozen base agent.\n126\tThe reward signal for the verification policy is based on \\emph{action change}: if verification changes the agent's action ($a_t' \\neq a_t$), the verification is deemed informative.\n127\tThe composite reward combines task success and action-change informativeness.\n128\t\n129\t\\subsection{Hypothesized Failure Mechanisms}\n130\t\n131\tWe hypothesize two mechanisms for step-independent failure in long-horizon tasks:\n132\t\n133\t\\paragraph{Policy information loss.}\n134\tA window of $h$ steps provides insufficient context for cross-step planning.\n135\tFor tasks requiring 30--45 steps, a window of $h{=}5$ captures only 11--17\\% of the trajectory.\n136\t\n137\t\\paragraph{Advantage sign misattribution.}\n138\tIn step-independent training, the advantage sign for window $w_i$ is determined primarily by the episode outcome rather than the quality of actions within $w_i$.\n139\tIn a failed episode, a locally good action receives negative advantage; in a successful episode, a locally poor action receives positive advantage.\n140\tThe GRPO group normalization may introduce partial local ranking signal, weakening this effect.\n141\t\n142\t\n143\t%% ============================================================\n144\t%% 4  EXPERIMENTS\n145\t%% ============================================================\n146\t\\section{Experiments}\n147\t\n148\tAll experiments use Qwen3-1.7B as the base model trained with GRPO on ALFWorld, unless otherwise noted.\n149\tTraining runs use 15 gradient steps with batch size 8 and 4 rollouts per prompt.\n150\tWe report validation success rate on a held-out set of 32 tasks.\n151\t\n152\t\\subsection{Context Window Ablation (h-Sweep)}\n153\t\n154\tWe sweep $h \\in \\{2, 5, 10, 20, \\text{full}\\}$ with 3 random seeds each to characterize how the step-independent performance cliff depends on window size.\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{table}[h]\n157\t\\centering\n158\t\\caption{Step-independent GRPO performance by context window size $h$ (3 seeds, 15 steps).}\n159\t\\label{tab:hsweep}\n160\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n161\t\\toprule\n162\t$h$ & Success (mean) & Std & Collapse Rate & Convergence Step \\\\\n163\t\\midrule\n164\t2    & 1.0\\%  & 1.8pp & 67\\% & 3--4 \\\\\n165\t5    & 11.5\\% & 10.4pp & 33\\% & 4--6 \\\\\n166\t10   & 14.6\\% & 6.3pp  & 0\\%  & N/A \\\\\n167\t20   & 19.8\\% & 4.8pp  & 0\\%  & N/A \\\\\n168\tfull & 25.0\\% & 3.1pp  & 0\\%  & N/A \\\\\n169\t\\bottomrule\n170\t\\end{tabular}\n171\t\\end{table}\n172\t\n173\tTable~\\ref{tab:hsweep} reveals a monotonic improvement with increasing $h$, but the transition from failure to success is not a sharp cliff.\n174\tAt $h{=}2$, two of three seeds collapse to 0\\% while one achieves 3.1\\%.\n175\tAt $h{=}5$, the picture is more complex: seed 2 collapses to 0\\%, but seeds 1 and 3 reach 15.6\\% and 18.8\\% respectively, yielding a standard deviation of 10.4pp.\n176\tThis high variance means the ``cliff location'' is a random variable, not a structural property of the algorithm.\n177\tAt $h{\\geq}10$, no seeds collapse, but performance remains well below episode-level (25.0\\%).\n178\tThe cliff threshold range is seed-dependent, falling between $h{=}3$ and $h{=}8$.\n179\t\n180\t\\subsection{Multi-Seed Episode-Level Replication}\n181\t\n182\tWe run 5 seeds of episode-level GRPO to establish its mean and variance.\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{table}[h]\n185\t\\centering\n186\t\\caption{Episode-level GRPO across 5 seeds. All seeds plateau by step 10--15.}\n187\t\\label{tab:multiseed}\n188\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n189\t\\toprule\n190\tSeed & Step 5 & Step 10 & Step 15 & Step 50 & Plateau Onset \\\\\n191\t\\midrule\n192\t1 & 25.0\\% & 25.0\\% & 28.1\\% & 28.1\\% & step 10 \\\\\n193\t2 & 18.8\\% & 18.8\\% & 18.8\\% & 21.9\\% & step 5 \\\\\n194\t3 & 25.0\\% & 31.3\\% & 31.3\\% & 31.3\\% & step 10 \\\\\n195\t4 & 12.5\\% & 15.6\\% & 15.6\\% & 15.6\\% & step 5 \\\\\n196\t5 & 18.8\\% & 25.0\\% & 25.0\\% & 28.1\\% & step 10 \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tMean & 20.0\\% & 23.1\\% & 23.8\\% & 25.0\\% & -- \\\\\n199\tStd  & 5.1pp  & 6.1pp  & 6.2pp  & 6.3pp  & -- \\\\\n200\t\\bottomrule\n201\t\\end{tabular}\n202\t\\end{table}\n203\t\n204\tThe mean success rate is 23.8\\% $\\pm$ 6.2pp at step 15 (Table~\\ref{tab:multiseed}).\n205\tAll seeds plateau between steps 5 and 10, and an additional 35 training steps (to step 50) yields only 1.2pp improvement (25.0\\%).\n206\tThis plateau is consistent with an information bottleneck: episode-level credit provides correct advantage direction but no per-token magnitude differentiation.\n207\tThe 95\\% confidence interval [11.3\\%, 36.3\\%] overlaps with step-independent $h{=}10$ (14.6\\%), weakening the statistical separability of these methods.\n208\t\n209\t\\subsection{GAE Baseline Comparison}\n210\t\n211\tWe compare episode-level GRPO against GAE with two configurations: $\\lambda{=}0.95, \\gamma{=}1.0$ (standard) and $\\lambda{=}1.0, \\gamma{=}1.0$ (Monte Carlo-like with critic baseline).\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[h]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\caption{Credit assignment method comparison (3 seeds, 15 steps).}\n216\t\\label{tab:gae}\n217\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n218\t\\toprule\n219\tMethod & Success & Std & $\\Delta$ vs. Episode & Valid Actions & Improving? \\\\\n220\t\\midrule\n221\tEpisode GRPO    & 23.8\\% & 6.2pp & --      & 55\\% & No (plateau) \\\\\n222\tGAE($\\lambda{=}1.0$)  & 37.5\\% & 5.5pp & +13.7pp & 72\\% & Marginal \\\\\n223\tGAE($\\lambda{=}0.95$) & 43.8\\% & 4.7pp & +20.0pp & 78\\% & Yes \\\\\n224\t\\bottomrule\n225\t\\end{tabular}\n226\t\\end{table}\n227\t\n228\tGAE($\\lambda{=}0.95$) outperforms episode-level GRPO by 20.0pp (Table~\\ref{tab:gae}), a substantial gap.\n229\tThis demonstrates that per-token temporal-difference credit is important: knowing the episode outcome is insufficient; the agent needs to know \\emph{which tokens contributed} to success or failure.\n230\tGAE also achieves higher action validity (78\\% vs. 55\\%) and continues improving at step 15, unlike episode-level GRPO which has plateaued.\n231\tThe GAE($\\lambda{=}1.0$) variant, equivalent to Monte Carlo returns with a critic baseline, achieves 37.5\\%, still 13.7pp above episode-level GRPO.\n232\tThese results confirm that finer credit assignment is necessary for strong performance in long-horizon agent tasks.\n233\t\n234\t\\subsection{Credit Assignment Mechanism Verification}\n235\t\n236\tWe empirically test the advantage sign misattribution hypothesis by logging per-window advantages and correlating them with episode outcomes and local action quality.\n237\t\n238\t\\begin{table}[h]\n239\t\\centering\n240\t\\caption{Advantage sign analysis: theoretical predictions vs. observed values.}\n241\t\\label{tab:mechanism}\n242\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n243\t\\toprule\n244\tMetric & Predicted & Observed & Interpretation \\\\\n245\t\\midrule\n246\tSign-outcome correlation   & $>$0.95 & 0.72 & Exists but not dominant \\\\\n247\tSign-local quality corr.   & $<$0.1  & 0.28 & Non-trivial local signal \\\\\n248\t$\\gamma^k$ decay $R^2$     & $>$0.8  & 0.45 & Weak fit \\\\\n249\tMixed-sign episodes         & 0\\%     & 18\\% & Theory violation \\\\\n250\tEpisode-level adv.\\ variance & 0       & 0    & Confirmed (trivially) \\\\\n251\t\\bottomrule\n252\t\\end{tabular}\n253\t\\end{table}\n254\t\n255\tTable~\\ref{tab:mechanism} shows that sign misattribution is a real phenomenon but weaker than predicted.\n256\tThe sign-outcome correlation of 0.72 confirms that advantage signs are biased toward episode outcomes, but 28\\% of the signal reflects local action quality.\n257\tThe 18\\% mixed-sign episodes violate the pure misattribution theory, which predicts 0\\%: GRPO's group normalization introduces within-group ranking that partially preserves local information.\n258\tThe $\\gamma^k$ magnitude decay shows only moderate fit ($R^2 = 0.45$), meaning advantage magnitudes decay with window position but noisily.\n259\tSign misattribution is \\emph{a contributing factor} to step-independent failure, not \\emph{the} explanation.\n260\t\n261\t\\subsection{Valid Action Bonus Ablation}\n262\t\n263\tWe test whether adding a small dense reward for action validity ($+$0.05 per valid action) can break the episode-level plateau.\n264\t\n265\tEpisode-level GRPO with the valid action bonus achieves 25.0\\% success at step 15 (vs. 23.8\\% vanilla), a negligible improvement.\n266\tHowever, action validity improves substantially: from 55\\% to 72\\% at step 15 and 85\\% at step 30.\n267\tThe agent learns to produce syntactically correct actions but not strategically better ones.\n268\tThe plateau is not caused by invalid action formatting; it stems from the inability of episode-level credit to differentiate among valid actions.\n269\tThis confirms that the bottleneck is credit assignment granularity, not reward sparsity.\n270\t\n271\t\\subsection{Selective Verification Results}\n272\t\n273\t\\subsubsection{Action-Change Statistics}\n274\t\n275\tBefore training the selective policy, we analyze whether action change after verification correlates with success in the base agent.\n276\tThe action change rate is 35\\%, with 65\\% of verifications being confirmatory.\n277\tThe correlation between action change and episode success is 0.08, and after controlling for policy entropy, the partial correlation drops to 0.03 ($p = 0.42$, not significant).\n278\tAction change in the low-entropy stratum correlates at 0.05, in the high-entropy stratum at 0.11.\n279\tThe entropy-success correlation is $-$0.15.\n280\tThese results indicate that action change is a side effect of high policy entropy, not a signal of verification informativeness.\n281\t\n282\tA causal intervention experiment comparing verification-triggered action changes against random changes at the same rate confirms this: verification-triggered changes yield 22\\% success vs. random changes at 19\\% ($\\Delta = 3$pp, $p = 0.31$, Cohen's $d = 0.12$).\n283\tAt 28\\% base success, the agent's ``correction'' is statistically indistinguishable from random re-sampling.\n284\t\n285\t\\subsubsection{B-IAVR Training}\n286\t\n287\t\\begin{table}[h]\n288\t\\centering\n289\t\\caption{B-IAVR selective verification training results.}\n290\t\\label{tab:biavr}\n291\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n292\t\\toprule\n293\tMetric & Target & Achieved & Gap \\\\\n294\t\\midrule\n295\tVerification rate reduction & $\\geq$40\\% & 12\\%   & $-$28pp \\\\\n296\tSuccess $\\Delta$ vs. always-on & $<$5pp   & $-$6.2pp & Fail \\\\\n297\tCost reduction               & $\\geq$40\\% & 12\\%   & $-$28pp \\\\\n298\tPolicy entropy (final)       & $>$0.3    & 0.08   & Collapsed \\\\\n299\t\\bottomrule\n300\t\\end{tabular}\n301\t\\end{table}\n302\t\n303\tThe B-IAVR policy degenerates to near-always-verify (88\\% verification rate, Table~\\ref{tab:biavr}).\n304\tThe policy entropy collapses to 0.08, indicating the policy learned a trivial solution: always verify with occasional random skips.\n305\tSuccess drops 6.2pp below always-on verification, and cost reduction is only 12\\%, far below the 40\\% target.\n306\tThe root cause is twofold: (1) the action-change reward signal is noise (Section 4.6.1), providing no learning gradient; (2) at 28\\% base success, any skip risks the already-fragile performance, making always-verify the locally optimal degenerate policy.\n307\t\n308\t\\subsubsection{Verification Strategy Comparison}\n309\t\n310\t\\begin{table}[h]\n311\t\\centering\n312\t\\caption{Verification strategy comparison across methods.}\n313\t\\label{tab:verifycomp}\n314\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n315\t\\toprule\n316\tMethod & Success & Verify Rate & Relative Cost & Success/Token \\\\\n317\t\\midrule\n318\tAlways-on         & 28.1\\% & 100\\% & 1.00$\\times$ & 0.281 \\\\\n319\tB-IAVR            & 21.9\\% & 88\\%  & 0.92$\\times$ & 0.238 \\\\\n320\tRandom ($p{=}0.5$) & 23.4\\% & 50\\%  & 0.67$\\times$ & 0.349 \\\\\n321\tConfidence        & 25.0\\% & 62\\%  & 0.74$\\times$ & 0.338 \\\\\n322\tNo verify         & 18.8\\% & 0\\%   & 0.45$\\times$ & 0.418 \\\\\n323\t\\bottomrule\n324\t\\end{tabular}\n325\t\\end{table}\n326\t\n327\tTable~\\ref{tab:verifycomp} shows B-IAVR is Pareto-dominated by every baseline.\n328\tRandom verification at $p{=}0.5$ achieves comparable success (23.4\\% vs. 21.9\\%) at nearly half the verification cost.\n329\tThe confidence-based heuristic (25.0\\% at 62\\% verification) also dominates B-IAVR.\n330\tThe no-verify condition drops only 9.3pp from always-on (28.1\\% to 18.8\\%), indicating that verification itself has limited value at this performance level: the agent's corrections are near-random perturbations.\n331\t\n332\t\\subsubsection{Reward Signal Ablation}\n333\t\n334\t\\begin{table}[h]\n335\t\\centering\n336\t\\caption{Reward signal comparison for selective verification training.}\n337\t\\label{tab:rewardablation}\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\tReward Type & Success & Verify Rate & Pareto-Dominated? \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tAction-change  & 21.9\\% & 88\\% & Yes (by all others) \\\\\n343\tRandom trigger & 23.4\\% & 52\\% & No \\\\\n344\tConfidence     & 25.0\\% & 65\\% & No \\\\\n345\t\\bottomrule\n346\t\\end{tabular}\n347\t\\end{table}\n348\t\n349\tThe action-change reward, our proposed intrinsic signal, produces the worst selective policy (Table~\\ref{tab:rewardablation}).\n350\tIt trains a near-always-verify policy because the reward is noise (partial correlation with success: 0.03 after entropy control).\n351\tRandom triggering learns a more balanced skip rate because uniform random reward at least does not actively mislead.\n352\tConfidence-based reward performs best, as model confidence has weak but real correlation with success.\n353\t\n354\t\\subsubsection{ARES Comparison}\n355\t\n356\tARES, which uses supervised learning from oracle verification decisions, achieves 25.0\\% success with 55\\% verification rate and 38\\% cost reduction.\n357\tB-IAVR achieves 21.9\\% at 88\\% verification rate with 12\\% cost reduction.\n358\tARES dominates B-IAVR on all metrics.\n359\tThis is expected: supervised learning from oracle labels does not require a meaningful RL reward signal, bypassing the core bottleneck that causes B-IAVR to fail.\n360\tThe result suggests that SFT-based verification routing is more practical than RL-based approaches when base agent competence is low.\n361\t\n362\t\\subsection{Transfer and Scaling}\n363\t\n364\t\\subsubsection{ScienceWorld Transfer}\n365\t\n366\t\\begin{table}[h]\n367\t\\centering\n368\t\\caption{ScienceWorld transfer: episode-level vs. step-independent.}\n369\t\\label{tab:scienceworld}\n370\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n371\t\\toprule\n372\tMethod & Success & Std & Avg Episode Length \\\\\n373\t\\midrule\n374\tEpisode-level         & 15.0\\% & 6pp & 12.3 steps \\\\\n375\tStep-independent ($h{=}5$) & 12.0\\% & 8pp & 12.3 steps \\\\\n376\t\\bottomrule\n377\t\\end{tabular}\n378\t\\end{table}\n379\t\n380\tOn ScienceWorld (Table~\\ref{tab:scienceworld}), episode-level GRPO achieves only a 3pp advantage over step-independent ($p = 0.38$, not significant).\n381\tScienceWorld episodes average 12.3 steps, compared to 15--45 in ALFWorld.\n382\tWith shorter episodes, step-independent windows capture a larger fraction of the trajectory, reducing information loss and sign misattribution.\n383\tThis result indicates that the step-independent disadvantage is \\emph{horizon-dependent}: it manifests primarily in long-episode environments.\n384\t\n385\t\\subsubsection{7B Model Scaling}\n386\t\n387\t\\begin{table}[h]\n388\t\\centering\n389\t\\caption{Scaling to Qwen3-7B: episode-level vs. step-independent.}\n390\t\\label{tab:scaling}\n391\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n392\t\\toprule\n393\tMethod & 1.7B & 7B & Gap Reduction \\\\\n394\t\\midrule\n395\tEpisode-level            & 28.1\\% & 35\\% & -- \\\\\n396\tStep-independent ($h{=}5$) & 0\\%    & 22\\% & 28pp $\\to$ 13pp \\\\\n397\t\\bottomrule\n398\t\\end{tabular}\n399\t\\end{table}\n400\t\n401\tAt 7B scale (Table~\\ref{tab:scaling}), step-independent training no longer collapses: it reaches 22\\% success at $h{=}5$, compared to 0\\% at 1.7B.\n402\tThe gap between episode-level and step-independent narrows from 28pp to 13pp.\n403\tLarger models have stronger in-context learning capabilities, partially compensating for the limited context window in step-independent training.\n404\tThis suggests that the ``catastrophic failure'' of step-independent training is specific to smaller models on long-horizon tasks.\n405\t\n406\t\\subsubsection{Per-Task Breakdown}\n407\t\n408\t\\begin{table}[h]\n409\t\\centering\n410\t\\caption{Per-task-type breakdown (episode-level GRPO, 1.7B).}\n411\t\\label{tab:pertask}\n412\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n413\t\\toprule\n414\tTask Type & Episode-Level & Step-Indep.\\ ($h{=}5$) & Avg Length \\\\\n415\t\\midrule\n416\tPick       & 35\\% & 8\\%  & 15 steps \\\\\n417\tHeat       & 30\\% & 3\\%  & 22 steps \\\\\n418\tClean      & 25\\% & 0\\%  & 28 steps \\\\\n419\tCool       & 20\\% & 2\\%  & 25 steps \\\\\n420\tExamine    & 15\\% & 0\\%  & 38 steps \\\\\n421\tPick\\_two  & 10\\% & 0\\%  & 45 steps \\\\\n422\t\\bottomrule\n423\t\\end{tabular}\n424\t\\end{table}\n425\t\n426\tTable~\\ref{tab:pertask} shows that both episode-level success and the episode-level vs. step-independent gap correlate with task length (correlation 0.45 with episode length).\n427\tShorter tasks (Pick, 15 steps) are easiest; longer multi-object tasks (Pick\\_two, 45 steps) are hardest.\n428\tStep-independent training fails completely on tasks longer than 25 steps.\n429\tHowever, the moderate correlation (0.45) indicates that task complexity, not length alone, drives difficulty.\n430\t\n431\t\n432\t%% ============================================================\n433\t%% 5  DISCUSSION\n434\t%% ============================================================\n435\t\\section{Discussion}\n436\t\n437\t\\paragraph{The coarser-is-sufficient hypothesis is false.}\n438\tOur original hypothesis was that episode-level credit assignment, while coarser than per-token methods, would suffice for training effective multi-turn agents.\n439\tThe 20pp gap between episode-level GRPO and GAE($\\lambda{=}0.95$) definitively refutes this.\n440\tEpisode-level training provides correct advantage direction (success vs. failure) but cannot differentiate token-level contributions, leading to an early plateau.\n441\t\n442\t\\paragraph{Sign misattribution is real but not dominant.}\n443\tWe find empirical support for the advantage sign misattribution mechanism (0.72 sign-outcome correlation), but it is weaker than theoretically predicted.\n444\tGRPO's group normalization introduces partial local ranking that our theory did not account for, resulting in 18\\% mixed-sign episodes and a non-trivial 0.28 correlation with local action quality.\n445\tSign misattribution is one factor in step-independent failure, alongside information loss and $\\gamma$-decay, rather than the sole mechanism.\n446\t\n447\t\\paragraph{Selective verification has a competence prerequisite.}\n448\tThe complete failure of B-IAVR ($-$6.2pp success, 12\\% cost reduction, Pareto-dominated by all baselines) reveals a prerequisite not discussed in prior work: the base agent must be competent enough for verification to have causal value.\n449\tAt 28\\% success, verification-triggered action changes are statistically indistinguishable from random perturbations (3pp difference, $p = 0.31$).\n450\tWe conjecture that a base agent competence of $\\geq$50\\% is necessary for selective verification to be learnable via RL, though we cannot test this with our training setup.\n451\t\n452\t\\paragraph{Implications for the field.}\n453\tOur results suggest two actionable takeaways:\n454\t(1) Practitioners should not assume step-independent training will work for long-horizon agent tasks, particularly at small model scales, but the solution is finer credit (GAE or similar) rather than coarser episode-level training.\n455\t(2) Selective verification research should report base agent competence as a key variable and establish minimum competence thresholds before pursuing cost-efficiency optimizations.\n456\t\n457\t\n458\t%% ============================================================\n459\t%% 6  CONCLUSION\n460\t%% ============================================================\n461\t\\section{Conclusion}\n462\t\n463\tWe presented an empirical taxonomy of credit assignment failure modes in multi-turn agent RL, characterized across granularity, agent competence, and task horizon.\n464\tStep-independent training exhibits a stochastic, seed-dependent performance cliff that is most severe at small model scales and long task horizons.\n465\tEpisode-level GRPO avoids collapse but plateaus significantly below GAE-based training, demonstrating that per-token credit differentiation is necessary.\n466\tSelective self-verification fails at base agent competence below 30\\%, revealing a prerequisite bottleneck.\n467\tThese negative results constrain the design space for future work: neither ``coarser credit suffices'' nor ``learn when to verify on a weak agent'' are viable research directions, while the interaction between model scale, task horizon, and credit granularity merits systematic investigation.\n468\t\n469\t\\subsection*{Limitations}\n470\t\n471\tOur study is limited to ALFWorld and ScienceWorld with models up to 7B parameters.\n472\tThe step-independent failure may not generalize to environments with denser reward signals or shorter episodes.\n473\tThe multi-seed analysis uses 3--5 seeds, which provides limited statistical power for characterizing distributional properties.\n474\tThe GAE results use a simple value function architecture; more sophisticated critics may further widen the gap.\n475\tWe could not test the conjectured 50\\% competence threshold for selective verification because our training setup does not reach that level.\n476\t\n477\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n478\t\\bibliography{references}\n479\t\n480\t\\end{document}\n481\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XS4LZkjt62xaP1Q64ZRwjr","session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"ee83cabd-5b98-45a2-99ab-c9533433b17c","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:08:38.212Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015ZxGepLG52S7nxBFegeEVH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01L5kNHV22PF5xW4os2Gmevn","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_1/projects/cost-verify-agent-rl/artifacts/_project/paper/theoretical_analysis.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32470,"cache_read_input_tokens":22398,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":32470,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XS4LZkjt62xaP1Q64ZRwjr","session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"e9f8ff41-e3cb-4d1e-862a-dc4ea530934e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L5kNHV22PF5xW4os2Gmevn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Theoretical Analysis}\n2\t\\label{sec:theory}\n3\t\n4\tSection~\\ref{sec:dual_mechanism} identified two failure mechanisms of step-independent training: policy information loss and advantage sign misattribution. We now formalize why sign misattribution is the more fundamental failure, derive the probability of incorrect gradient direction as a function of task success rate, examine why the community has defaulted to step-independent training, and connect our findings to the classical credit assignment literature.\n5\t\n6\t\\subsection{Why Sign Misattribution is Fundamental}\n7\t\\label{sec:sign_fundamental}\n8\t\n9\tThe two mechanisms identified in Section~\\ref{sec:dual_mechanism} produce qualitatively different optimization errors. Policy information loss (Mechanism 1) reduces the information available to each window's policy, producing suboptimal but directionally reasonable gradient updates. Sign misattribution (Mechanism 2) corrupts the gradient direction itself. We formalize this distinction below.\n10\t\n11\t\\textbf{Proposition 1.} \\textit{Under step-independent training with binary episode reward $R_{\\text{episode}} \\in \\{0, r_+\\}$, the probability that window $k$'s advantage $\\hat{A}_k$ has the correct sign (aligned with local action quality) approaches the base success rate $p$ as the number of rollouts $K$ per group increases.}\n12\t\n13\t\\textit{Argument.} Define ``correct sign'' as follows: for a window containing locally beneficial actions, the correct sign of $\\hat{A}_k$ is positive (reinforcement); for a window containing locally detrimental actions, the correct sign is negative (suppression). Under binary reward, the return for window $k$ is $R_k = \\gamma^{(N-k)} \\cdot R_{\\text{episode}}$ (Equation~\\ref{eq:step_return}). In a failed episode ($R_{\\text{episode}} = 0$), all windows receive $R_k = 0$ regardless of their local action quality. If the group contains at least one successful rollout, the group mean $\\mu > 0$, and all windows in the failed episode receive negative advantages after normalization via Equation~\\ref{eq:grpo_advantage}. This includes windows where the agent took locally correct actions, such as correctly identifying a target object in an exploration step. Now consider a window with good local actions. In a successful episode (probability $p$), the window receives positive $\\hat{A}_k$, which is the correct sign. In a failed episode (probability $1 - p$), the window receives negative $\\hat{A}_k$ whenever $\\mu > 0$, which is the wrong sign. The probability that $\\mu = 0$ (all $K$ rollouts in the group failed) is $(1-p)^K$, which shrinks exponentially with $K$. As $K$ grows, nearly every failed episode produces a non-zero group mean, and the window receives the wrong sign with probability approaching $1-p$. The probability of correct sign therefore approaches $p$. \\qed\n14\t\n15\tThe distinction between sign error and magnitude error clarifies why Mechanism 2 is catastrophic while Mechanism 1 is merely suboptimal. The discount factor $\\gamma^{(N-k)}$ in Equation~\\ref{eq:step_return} introduces magnitude decay across window positions: for an episode with $T=50$ steps and $h=2$, the first window ($k=1$) retains $\\gamma^{N-1} = 0.95^{24} \\approx 0.29$ of the terminal window's return. This attenuation reduces the gradient magnitude for early windows but preserves the gradient direction. A gradient with correct sign and reduced magnitude produces slow convergence, a condition recoverable through longer training. A gradient with wrong sign produces active divergence: the optimization pushes the policy away from good behaviors and toward bad ones. Consider a navigation analogy. Magnitude error corresponds to a compass that points correctly but displays a dimmed needle; the traveler moves slowly toward the destination. Sign error corresponds to a compass pointing in the opposite direction; the traveler walks away from the destination with full confidence. The first error is correctable by patience. The second is correctable only by replacing the compass.\n16\t\n17\t\\subsection{Formal Probability Analysis}\n18\t\\label{sec:formal_probability}\n19\t\n20\tWe derive a tighter bound on the probability of correct gradient direction as a function of task difficulty and group size.\n21\t\n22\t\\textbf{Proposition 2.} \\textit{Let $p$ denote the episode success rate and $K$ the number of rollouts per group. Under step-independent training with binary episode reward, the probability that a gradient update for a window with locally good actions has the correct sign satisfies:}\n23\t\\begin{equation}\n24\tP(\\text{correct sign or no update}) = p + (1-p)^{K}\n25\t\\label{eq:correct_bound}\n26\t\\end{equation}\n27\t\\textit{Among gradient updates that occur (non-zero advantage), the fraction with correct sign is:}\n28\t\\begin{equation}\n29\tP(\\text{correct} \\mid \\text{update}) = \\frac{p}{1 - (1-p)^{K}}\n30\t\\label{eq:correct_conditional}\n31\t\\end{equation}\n32\t\n33\t\\textit{Derivation.} For a window with locally good actions, the gradient direction depends on $\\text{sign}(\\hat{A}_k)$, which depends on whether the episode succeeded and whether the group contains any successful rollouts. Three mutually exclusive cases arise.\n34\t\n35\t\\textit{Case 1: Episode succeeded} (probability $p$). The window receives $R_k = \\gamma^{(N-k)} \\cdot r_+ > 0$. With at least one failed rollout in the group, $R_k > \\mu$ after normalization, producing $\\hat{A}_k > 0$. The gradient reinforces the window's actions. For a window with good local actions, this is the correct direction.\n36\t\n37\t\\textit{Case 2: Episode failed, group contains at least one success} (probability $(1-p)[1 - (1-p)^{K-1}]$). The window receives $R_k = 0 < \\mu$, producing $\\hat{A}_k < 0$. The gradient suppresses the window's actions. For a window with good local actions, this is the wrong direction.\n38\t\n39\t\\textit{Case 3: Episode failed, entire group failed} (probability $(1-p) \\cdot (1-p)^{K-1} = (1-p)^{K}$). All returns are zero, so $\\mu = 0$ and $\\sigma = 0$. The advantage is zero, producing no gradient update.\n40\t\n41\tSumming Cases~1 and~3 yields Equation~\\ref{eq:correct_bound}. Since only Cases~1 and~2 produce non-zero gradients, the conditional probability follows as Equation~\\ref{eq:correct_conditional}.\n42\t\n43\tWe evaluate these expressions at the empirical baseline of our experiments. At $p = 6.25\\%$ (the pre-training success rate) and $K = 4$: $(1-p)^K = 0.9375^4 \\approx 0.77$, giving $P(\\text{correct or no update}) \\approx 0.83$. Of the 23\\% of cases that produce a gradient update, only $P(\\text{correct} \\mid \\text{update}) = 0.0625 / 0.23 \\approx 27\\%$ have the correct sign. As $K$ increases to 16, $(1-p)^{K} \\approx 0.36$, and $P(\\text{correct} \\mid \\text{update}) \\approx 9.8\\%$. In the large-$K$ limit, $P(\\text{correct} \\mid \\text{update}) \\to p$, so approximately $1 - p \\approx 94\\%$ of all gradient updates for locally good windows point in the wrong direction. This is consistent with the empirical observation that step-independent training collapses to 0\\% task success (exp026, exp028c) while episode-level training achieves stable improvement (exp029, 28.125\\% validation success at step 15).\n44\t\n45\t\\subsection{Why the Community Defaults to Step-Independent Training}\n46\t\\label{sec:why_defaults}\n47\t\n48\tIf step-independent training is fundamentally flawed for multi-turn agents, why has it become the default? We identify six contributing factors.\n49\t\n50\t\\textbf{1. Context window constraints.} Smaller language models (1B to 4B parameters) typically support context windows of 2048 to 4096 tokens. Multi-turn episodes spanning 50 or more interaction steps easily exceed these limits. Step-independent windowing is a pragmatic response to a hardware constraint, not a deliberate algorithmic choice.\n51\t\n52\t\\textbf{2. Token-level training inertia.} Language model training is organized around predicting the next token over fixed-length sequences. When multi-turn RL inherits this paradigm, the natural unit of optimization becomes a fixed-length window rather than a variable-length episode. The mismatch between token-level optimization and episode-level credit assignment goes unquestioned because it aligns with existing training infrastructure.\n53\t\n54\t\\textbf{3. Framework design accident.} The RAGEN framework~\\cite{ragen2025} implements step-independent mode as its default configuration, not as one option among alternatives. Users inherit this choice without an explicit decision point, and default configurations propagate silently through downstream projects.\n55\t\n56\t\\textbf{4. Memory and compute constraints.} Episode-level training requires processing $O(T)$ tokens per sequence, incurring $O(T^2)$ attention cost with full self-attention. Step-independent training reduces this to $O(h)$ tokens and $O(h^2)$ attention cost per window. The computational savings are immediate and measurable, while the credit assignment failure is subtle and deferred until training collapse occurs.\n57\t\n58\t\\textbf{5. Reward shaping as apparent fix.} Dense reward shaping (per-step bonuses for intermediate goals) appears to address sparse reward problems and has theoretical support in the single-agent RL literature. Our experiment with dense rewards under step-independent training (exp013f) reveals a different failure mode: the agent learns to maximize format-level rewards (action validity rises from 60\\% to 97.5\\%) while task success remains at 0\\%. Reward shaping does not resolve sign misattribution; it creates new optimization targets that the agent can game without solving the underlying task.\n59\t\n60\t\\textbf{6. Absence of negative results.} When step-independent training fails, practitioners attribute the failure to insufficient hyperparameter tuning or the inherent difficulty of sparse rewards. Without published analyses that isolate the credit assignment mechanism as the root cause, the community cannot distinguish between ``this problem is hard'' and ``this training paradigm is broken for this class of problems.''\n61\t\n62\t\\subsection{Connection to Classical Credit Assignment}\n63\t\\label{sec:classical_connection}\n64\t\n65\tOur findings connect to a long line of work on temporal credit assignment in reinforcement learning. We position the step-independent failure mode within this literature and clarify the contribution of our analysis.\n66\t\n67\t\\textbf{Temporal difference learning and eligibility traces.} Sutton's TD($\\lambda$)~\\cite{sutton1988td} provides a spectrum of credit assignment strategies parameterized by $\\lambda \\in [0,1]$. At $\\lambda = 0$, credit is assigned via one-step bootstrapped returns. At $\\lambda = 1$, TD($\\lambda$) reduces to Monte Carlo returns, where each state receives the full episode return. Step-independent training falls below this spectrum: windows are disconnected from each other, so temporal propagation of credit does not occur. Each window receives an independently computed advantage with no mechanism for information to flow across windows within the same episode.\n68\t\n69\t\\textbf{Generalized Advantage Estimation.} GAE($\\lambda, \\gamma$)~\\cite{schulman2016gae} extends TD($\\lambda$) with a separate discount factor for the advantage estimator. GAE(0, $\\gamma$) produces per-step advantages through a learned value function. GAE(1, 1) produces undiscounted Monte Carlo returns, and is equivalent to episode-level GRPO without a critic. The key difference is that GAE uses value function subtraction as the baseline for variance reduction, while GRPO uses group normalization. Episode-level GRPO can be viewed as GAE(1, 1) with a group-relative baseline replacing the learned value function. This substitution is effective when the group provides a representative reference distribution, but it does not address per-step credit assignment within an episode.\n70\t\n71\t\\textbf{REINFORCE.} Williams' REINFORCE algorithm~\\cite{williams1992reinforce} computes policy gradients from complete episode returns with a learned baseline $b(s)$. Episode-level GRPO is equivalent to REINFORCE where the group mean $\\mu$ replaces $b(s)$ and the group standard deviation $\\sigma$ provides normalization. The algorithmic structure is not new. Our contribution is the diagnosis: the community's standard adaptation of this algorithm for multi-turn LLM agents (step-independent windowing) silently breaks the credit assignment structure that REINFORCE assumes.\n72\t\n73\t\\textbf{Multi-agent credit assignment analogy.} Step-independent windows can be understood through cooperative multi-agent games. Each window acts as an independent ``agent'' sharing a team reward (the episode outcome) but optimized without knowledge of other windows' actions. The sign misattribution problem parallels team reward credit assignment in cooperative multi-agent RL: when the team fails, all agents receive identical penalties regardless of individual contribution. In multi-agent RL, this is a recognized problem with known solutions such as counterfactual baselines. In multi-turn LLM agent training, the same structural problem arises from an implementation choice and has not been recognized as a credit assignment failure.\n74\t\n75\tTo summarize our positioning: we do not propose a new credit assignment algorithm. Episode-level training is the standard approach, and GRPO was designed as an episode-level method. What we identify is that the community's adaptation of GRPO to multi-turn settings introduces a systematic sign error in gradient computation. This error is not a gradual performance degradation but a catastrophic failure mode, as confirmed by the contrast between step-independent collapse (exp026, exp028c: 0\\% success) and episode-level stability (exp029: 28.125\\% validation success from a 12.5\\% baseline).\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XS4LZkjt62xaP1Q64ZRwjr","session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"077e75f3-bf74-4ad2-8179-7888fc93ed4d","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:08:43.431Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015ZxGepLG52S7nxBFegeEVH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016TJM2CqW28MiHfN8Qtfy1d","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_1/projects/cost-verify-agent-rl/artifacts/_project/paper/experiments.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32470,"cache_read_input_tokens":22398,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":32470,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XS4LZkjt62xaP1Q64ZRwjr","session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"d51f5129-d924-48bc-9f5e-779a16adab14"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016TJM2CqW28MiHfN8Qtfy1d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\newcommand{\\placeholder}[1]{\\textcolor{red}{[#1]}}\n2\t\n3\t\\section{Experiments}\n4\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n5\t\n6\tWe design experiments to answer three questions. First, does step-independent training collapse as predicted by our dual mechanism analysis, and does episode-level training avoid this collapse? Second, how does the window size $h$ modulate the severity of failure, and is there a credit assignment cliff below some critical threshold? Third, can we directly measure advantage sign misattribution during training to validate the theoretical predictions of Section~\\ref{sec:m2_sign_misattribution}?\n7\t\n8\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n9\t\\label{sec:experimental_setup}\n10\t\n11\t\\paragraph{Environment.}\n12\tWe evaluate on ALFWorld~\\cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, a text-based household environment where an agent interacts with a simulated kitchen, bedroom, and living room through natural language commands. % TODO: add shridhar2021alfworld to .bib\n13\tALFWorld provides six task types (pick, clean, heat, cool, examine, pick two) that require sequential reasoning over multiple interaction steps. The agent issues text commands (e.g., ``go to countertop 1'', ``take knife 2 from drawer 3'') and receives textual observations describing the environment state. Episodes terminate after task completion or a maximum of 50 interaction steps. The reward is binary: $R = 1$ for successful task completion and $R = 0$ for failure. This binary reward structure makes ALFWorld a particularly challenging testbed for credit assignment: the only learning signal arrives at episode termination, and intermediate steps receive no direct feedback.\n14\t\n15\tALFWorld is well-suited for studying the step-independent failure because its tasks exhibit strong sequential dependencies. A typical task requires the agent to locate an object (steps 1--5), pick it up (step 6), navigate to a target receptacle (steps 7--10), and place the object (step 11). With step-independent windowing at $h = 2$, the window containing the placement action has no access to the exploration steps that determined which object to pick up.\n16\t\n17\t\\paragraph{Base model.}\n18\tWe use Qwen3-1.7B (instruct variant) as the policy model. Before RL training, we perform supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on 2546 expert demonstrations collected from ALFWorld, training for 2 epochs with learning rate $5 \\times 10^{-6}$. The resulting SFT checkpoint achieves approximately 6.25\\% success rate on a held-out 32-episode validation set, providing a minimal but non-trivial starting point for RL. This low baseline success rate is important for our analysis: at $p = 0.0625$ with group size $K = 8$, Proposition 2 (Section~\\ref{sec:formal_probability}) predicts that approximately 84.5\\% of gradient updates carry incorrect sign under step-independent training.\n19\t\n20\t\\paragraph{RL training configuration.}\n21\tWe train using the RAGEN framework~\\cite{ragen2025} with the GRPO algorithm. The shared hyperparameters across all conditions are:\n22\t\\begin{itemize}\n23\t    \\item Group size $K = 8$ (8 rollouts per task instance for advantage estimation)\n24\t    \\item Learning rate: $1 \\times 10^{-6}$\n25\t    \\item KL penalty coefficient: $\\beta = 0.01$ (low-variance KL)\n26\t    \\item Discount factor: $\\gamma = 0.95$ (applied in step-independent return computation via Equation~\\ref{eq:step_return}; not applicable to episode-level mode)\n27\t    \\item Invalid action penalty coefficient: 0.1\n28\t    \\item Training data: 16 task instances per training step\n29\t    \\item Training steps: 15 (early-stopped based on convergence)\n30\t\\end{itemize}\n31\t\n32\t\\paragraph{Evaluation protocol.}\n33\tWe evaluate on a fixed 32-episode validation set every 5 training steps. The primary metric is success rate (pass@1): the fraction of episodes where the agent completes the task within 50 steps. We also report pass@$k$ ($k \\in \\{2, 4, 8\\}$), computed by sampling $k$ rollouts per episode and counting success if any rollout succeeds. As a diagnostic metric, we track \\textit{action validity rate}: the fraction of agent outputs that are syntactically valid environment commands (as opposed to malformed text, repeated outputs, or empty responses). Action validity serves as a proxy for basic policy coherence, distinct from task-solving capability. All reported numbers are single-seed unless otherwise noted; multi-seed results (3 seeds per condition) are marked as pending where applicable.\n34\t\n35\t\\subsection{Window-Size Ablation}\n36\t\\label{sec:window_ablation}\n37\t\n38\tTo disentangle the effects of information loss (Mechanism 1) and sign misattribution (Mechanism 2) across different context lengths, we conduct a systematic ablation over window size $h$.\n39\t\n40\t\\paragraph{Conditions.}\n41\tWe vary the step-independent history length $h \\in \\{2, 5, 10, 20\\}$ and compare against episode-level training (equivalent to $h = T$, the full trajectory). Each condition uses the \\texttt{step\\_independent} training mode with \\texttt{step\\_independent\\_history\\_length} $= h$, except the episode-level condition which processes the complete trajectory as a single GRPO sample.\n42\t\n43\t\\paragraph{Design rationale.}\n44\tThe five conditions span the spectrum from minimal context ($h = 2$, producing $N = 25$ windows per episode at $T = 50$) to full context ($h = T$, one sample per episode). Theory predicts two observable effects. Mechanism 1 (information loss) produces a performance ceiling that varies with $h$: smaller windows restrict the representable strategy space more severely, so step-1 performance should decrease monotonically with $h$. Mechanism 2 (sign misattribution) produces a training-time degradation that is present for all $h < T$: as long as windows exist, the advantage sign is determined by episode outcome rather than local action quality, so performance should decline over training steps regardless of window size. The combined prediction is a credit assignment cliff: performance drops sharply below a critical $h$ value, with smaller $h$ exhibiting both lower initial performance and faster collapse.\n45\t\n46\t\\paragraph{Controls.}\n47\tAll conditions share the same GRPO hyperparameters (group size, learning rate, KL penalty, discount factor). Each condition runs for 15 training steps with validation every 5 steps. We plan 3 seeds per $h$ value to assess variance. \\placeholder{plan\\_002: 3-seed h-sweep execution status. Currently single-seed for h=2 (exp028c) and episode-level (exp029).}\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{Post-hoc evaluation.}\n50\tTo isolate the training-layer effect of sign misattribution from the inference-layer effect of context truncation, we conduct full-context evaluation of checkpoints trained with truncated windows. Concretely, a model trained with $h = 2$ is evaluated using the complete interaction history at inference time. If the trained model performs better under full-context evaluation than under $h = 2$ evaluation, the difference reflects pure inference-time information loss (Mechanism 1). If it still performs worse than the episode-level trained model even under full-context evaluation, the gap reflects training-layer damage from sign misattribution (Mechanism 2).\n51\t\n52\t\\subsection{Results}\n53\t\\label{sec:results}\n54\t\n55\t\\subsubsection{Episode-Level vs. Step-Independent Training}\n56\t\\label{sec:results_main}\n57\t\n58\tThe central empirical finding is the contrast between episode-level and step-independent training. Episode-level GRPO achieves 28.125\\% validation success at training step 15, a 4.5$\\times$ improvement over the SFT baseline of approximately 6.25\\%. Step-independent training with $h = 2$ collapses to 0\\% validation success by step 10.\n59\t\n60\t\\placeholder{tab1\\_main\\_results: Episode-level vs step-independent comparison. Columns: Method, $h$-value, Val Success (\\%), Action Valid (\\%), Pass@8. Multi-seed mean$\\pm$std from plan\\_003.}\n61\t\n62\t\\placeholder{fig6\\_training\\_curves: Training curves showing episode-level vs step-independent $h=2$. Dual y-axis: success rate (left) and action validity (right). X-axis: training steps 0--15.}\n63\t\n64\tThe collapse under step-independent training follows the two-phase pattern predicted by our analysis. In the first phase (steps 0--2), task success drops from 18.75\\% to single digits while action validity remains high at approximately 89.3\\%. The model retains basic command formatting but loses the ability to compose multi-step strategies. In the second phase (steps 2--4), action validity itself collapses from 89.3\\% to 43.1\\%. Accumulated wrong-sign gradient updates, which initially eroded only strategic capability, propagate to corrupt the action space representation. By step 10, the agent produces predominantly malformed outputs and achieves no task completions.\n65\t\n66\tEpisode-level training exhibits no such collapse. Action validity remains above 95\\% throughout training, and success rate increases monotonically. The single advantage shared across all steps in an episode preserves correct actions even within failed episodes (since all steps receive the same sign, no individual step is singled out for incorrect suppression). Cross-step dependencies are maintained through the full interaction history, so the model can learn conditional strategies that require information from early exploration steps.\n67\t\n68\t\\subsubsection{Window-Size Ablation}\n69\t\\label{sec:results_hsweep}\n70\t\n71\t\\placeholder{tab2\\_h\\_sweep: $h$-sweep ablation results. Columns: $h$, Seed 1/2/3, Mean$\\pm$Std, Collapse Step (first step where val success $= 0$). Rows: $h \\in \\{2, 5, 10, 20, \\text{full}\\}$, 3 seeds per $h$.}\n72\t\n73\t\\placeholder{fig5\\_h\\_sweep\\_curve: $h$ vs. validation success at step 15. Error bars from 3 seeds. Shows credit assignment cliff. Horizontal dashed line at SFT baseline (6.25\\%).}\n74\t\n75\tThe ablation reveals a credit assignment cliff consistent with our theoretical predictions. We describe the expected pattern below; precise numbers await multi-seed completion.\n76\t\n77\tThe dual mechanism theory predicts three observable signatures in the $h$-sweep. First, step-1 performance should vary across $h$ values, reflecting different information-loss ceilings (Mechanism 1). Second, for all $h < T$, performance should decline over training steps as sign misattribution accumulates (Mechanism 2). Third, the rate of decline should be approximately constant across $h$ values (since the sign correlation from Equation~\\ref{eq:sign_correlation} depends on the success rate $p$ and group size $K$, not on $h$ directly), but the onset of visible collapse should be earlier for smaller $h$ because the lower ceiling leaves less room before reaching zero.\n78\t\n79\tFrom the single-seed data available, the two-phase collapse at $h = 2$ is confirmed. Action validity at step 2 is 89.3\\%, dropping to 43.1\\% by step 4. This pattern, strategic collapse preceding formatting collapse, is the empirical signature of sign misattribution operating on a model that initially retains valid action representations. The progressive nature distinguishes Mechanism 2 from Mechanism 1: information loss would produce a constant (low) performance from step 1, not a declining trajectory.\n80\t\n81\t\\subsubsection{Dense Reward Baseline}\n82\t\\label{sec:results_dense}\n83\t\n84\tA natural hypothesis is that step-independent training fails due to sparse reward, and that providing dense per-step rewards might resolve the credit assignment problem. We test this with a dense reward configuration that adds $+0.15$ bonus for each syntactically valid action, applied under step-independent training with $h = 2$.\n85\t\n86\tThe dense reward experiment (exp013f) produces a striking dissociation. Action validity increases from 60\\% to 97.5\\% over training. The per-step bonus successfully shapes low-level formatting behavior. However, task success remains at 0\\% throughout training. The agent learns to produce perfectly formatted commands that satisfy the validity reward without solving any tasks.\n87\t\n88\tThis result confirms that the failure of step-independent training is not a sparse reward problem. Dense reward shaping creates an optimization target that the agent can maximize (action formatting) without addressing the underlying credit assignment pathology. The sign of the task-level advantage still corrupts multi-step strategy learning, while the dense reward merely teaches the agent to produce syntactically correct (but strategically meaningless) outputs. Reward shaping and sign misattribution operate on different dimensions of behavior and cannot compensate for each other.\n89\t\n90\t\\subsection{Empirical Verification of Sign Misattribution}\n91\t\\label{sec:sign_verification}\n92\t\n93\tThe theoretical analysis of Section~\\ref{sec:m2_sign_misattribution} predicts specific quantitative properties of the advantage signal under step-independent training. We design a direct measurement protocol to validate these predictions.\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Methodology.}\n96\tDuring step-independent training, we log per-window advantage information for each training episode. For window $k$ in episode $i$, we record: (1) the advantage sign $\\text{sign}(\\hat{A}_k^{(i)})$, (2) the episode outcome $R_{\\text{episode}}^{(i)} \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, and (3) a local action quality proxy based on the fraction of valid actions within the window. We then compute three statistics across all training windows:\n97\t\\begin{itemize}\n98\t    \\item \\textbf{Sign-outcome correlation}: the Pearson correlation between $\\text{sign}(\\hat{A}_k)$ and $R_{\\text{episode}}$. Theory predicts this correlation approaches 1.0, since the advantage sign is determined almost entirely by whether the episode succeeded.\n99\t    \\item \\textbf{Sign-quality correlation}: the Pearson correlation between $\\text{sign}(\\hat{A}_k)$ and local action quality. Theory predicts this correlation approaches 0, since the advantage sign carries no information about whether the window's actions were locally correct.\n100\t    \\item \\textbf{Correct sign fraction}: $P(\\text{correct sign} \\mid \\text{update})$, defined as the probability that a window with locally good actions receives a positive advantage and a window with locally bad actions receives a negative advantage. Proposition 2 (Section~\\ref{sec:formal_probability}) predicts this equals approximately 15.5\\% at $p = 6.25\\%$, $K = 8$.\n101\t\\end{itemize}\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{Predictions.}\n104\tThe sign misattribution mechanism makes three testable predictions:\n105\t\\begin{enumerate}\n106\t    \\item Sign-outcome correlation $\\approx 1.0$ (advantage sign is a proxy for episode success, not local quality)\n107\t    \\item Sign-quality correlation $\\approx 0$ (advantage sign is independent of whether local actions were good)\n108\t    \\item $P(\\text{correct sign}) \\approx 15.5\\%$ (derived from Proposition 2 at the observed success rate)\n109\t\\end{enumerate}\n110\tIf all three predictions are confirmed empirically, the sign misattribution mechanism is validated as the causal driver of training collapse, rather than alternative explanations such as reward sparsity, KL divergence, or optimization instability.\n111\t\n112\t\\placeholder{tab3\\_mechanism\\_verification: Sign misattribution empirical verification. Columns: Metric, Measured Value, Theoretical Prediction, Agreement. Rows: sign-outcome correlation, sign-quality correlation, $P(\\text{correct sign})$. Data from plan\\_004 per-window advantage logging.}\n113\t\n114\t\\subsection{Additional Baselines}\n115\t\\label{sec:additional_baselines}\n116\t\n117\t\\paragraph{GAE baselines.}\n118\tTo distinguish the effect of episode-level optimization from GRPO-specific design choices (group normalization, no value function), we compare against Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE)~\\cite{schulman2016gae} under two configurations. GAE($\\lambda = 0.95$, $\\gamma = 0.95$) provides standard TD-based advantage estimation with a learned value function, representing the conventional actor-critic approach to temporal credit assignment. GAE($\\lambda = 1$, $\\gamma = 1$) computes Monte Carlo returns equivalent to episode-level GRPO without group normalization, isolating the contribution of group-relative advantage computation. \\placeholder{plan\\_003b: GAE baseline execution. Columns: Method, Val Success (\\%), Action Valid (\\%), Training Stability (variance across steps).}\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{Selective verification.}\n121\tBuilding on the episode-level trained agent (Section~\\ref{sec:selective_verification}), we train a selective verification policy that decides when self-checking is informative. This component is orthogonal to the credit assignment analysis and demonstrates a downstream application of the competent base agent produced by episode-level training. \\placeholder{tab4\\_selective\\_comparison: Selective verification results. Columns: Method (Always-Verify, Never-Verify, Selective), Success (\\%), Verify Rate (\\%), Cost Reduction (\\%). Pending Phase B experiments.}\n122\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XS4LZkjt62xaP1Q64ZRwjr","session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"9b4c1ebf-23c5-413e-ae1d-745d409aba1d","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:08:43.680Z"}
