{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RhggbSeE7vwsgtQKjop7kR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012oGZBTTRmNaZcAJKrENLZ1","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Roundtable Steps 2-3: Cross-share and converge","prompt":"You are the Area Chair (AC) moderating a roundtable discussion between two reviewers of the paper \"Why Step-Independent RL Fails for LLM Multi-Turn Agents: Dual Mechanism Analysis\".\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1) — Focus: Methods + Experiments — Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept), Confidence 4/5\n\n### Key Points from R1:\n**Strengths identified:**\n- The sign attribution analysis (r=0.87 sign-vs-outcome, r=0.14 sign-vs-quality) is the paper's strongest quantitative result\n- h-sweep (5 values, 3 seeds each) is well-designed and shows clear monotonic degradation\n- Multi-seed replication (5 seeds, CI [28.4%, 40.4%]) convincingly establishes the qualitative claim\n- \"Necessary minimum\" framing is reasonable given the data\n- Tables are clear and informative\n- Narrative arc is logical and well-structured\n\n**Major Weaknesses:**\n1. No factorial mechanism separation — the two mechanisms (info loss + sign misattribution) are not experimentally isolated. Needs: episode-level advantage with truncated context to separate.\n2. Single environment (ALFWorld), single model scale (1.5B) — limits generality claims\n3. \"Catastrophic\" language overstates the h-sweep findings — only h=2 shows near-zero; h=5 is 7.3%, h=10 is 16.7%\n4. No formal significance tests for pairwise comparisons\n\n**Moderate Weaknesses:**\n5. GAE comparison at fixed step count (step 15), not convergence\n6. Local action quality proxy is coarse (action validity ≠ action quality)\n7. Selective verification section is weak and occupies a full section\n\n**Minor:**\n8. Related work gaps\n9. Small validation set (32 episodes, 3.125% resolution)\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- Can you provide a factorial experiment separating the two mechanisms?\n- How sensitive are sign attribution results to the local quality proxy?\n- What is GAE(0.95)'s converged performance?\n- GAGPO comparison: how to reconcile 34.4% with GAGPO's 93.5% at same scale?\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2) — Focus: Novelty + Related Work — Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence 4/5\n\n### Key Points from R2:\n**Strengths identified:**\n- Formal probability analysis is clean\n- Empirical verification is systematic\n- Median version is well-written\n- Title is appropriate for diagnostic paper\n\n**Major Weaknesses:**\n1. Limited novelty beyond Turn-PPO — \"uniform advantage\" to \"sign misattribution\" is a small conceptual jump\n2. Single environment, single scale (same as R1)\n3. Failure to disentangle the two mechanisms (same as R1)\n4. SOTA gap undermines relevance — GAGPO 93.5%, HiPER 97.4% at same/similar scale; diagnosing failure of a method nobody competitive uses has limited impact\n5. GAE comparison undermines \"coarser is sufficient\" narrative — 12.5pp gap shows finer credit provides substantial benefit\n\n**Moderate Weaknesses:**\n6. No head-to-head comparison with Turn-PPO's fix\n7. Credit assignment related work is thin — COMA, QMIX, difference rewards not cited\n8. Selective verification is preliminary/failed\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- Can you disentangle information loss from sign misattribution?\n- How does Turn-PPO's turn-level value function perform on ALFWorld?\n- What happens at 7B+?\n- Why care about diagnosing step-independent failure when GAGPO/HiPER already achieve 93-97%?\n- GAE outperforms episode-level by 12.5pp — doesn't this refute \"sufficient\"?\n\n---\n\n## Your Task: Run Steps 2 and 3 of the roundtable\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\nIdentify where the reviewers agree and disagree. Note areas where one reviewer raised a point the other didn't consider.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nFor each area of disagreement, determine which reviewer's assessment is more justified given the evidence. Arrive at a consensus assessment.\n\nProduce a structured output with:\n\n1. **Points of Agreement** (shared by both reviewers)\n2. **Points of Disagreement and Resolution**\n   - For each disagreement: state R1's position, R2's position, and your resolution with justification\n3. **Unique Contributions from Each Reviewer**\n   - Points R1 raised that R2 missed\n   - Points R2 raised that R1 missed\n4. **Consensus Strengths** (final agreed list)\n5. **Consensus Weaknesses** (final agreed list, ranked by severity)\n6. **Consensus Score** (with justification for why this score, not adjacent scores)\n7. **Key Revision Recommendations** (what would move this paper toward acceptance)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11387,"cache_read_input_tokens":63706,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11387},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"c07bbf81-44e9-4e6e-9853-9a4668a699d7"}
