{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tyo4FMaja5eQbcmFapQ8eU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n10\t\n11\t\\title{Why Step-Independent RL Fails for LLM Multi-Turn Agents:\\\\Dual Mechanism Analysis}\n12\t\n13\t\\author{Anonymous}\n14\t\n15\t\\date{}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t%==============================================================================\n21\t\\begin{abstract}\n22\t%==============================================================================\n23\tReinforcement Learning (RL) from environment rewards is a promising paradigm for training Large Language Model (LLM) agents on interactive tasks. However, current frameworks default to step-independent training, where each context window is treated as an independent optimization unit. We demonstrate that this default catastrophically fails on multi-turn tasks: agents trained with step-independent Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) achieve near-zero success on ALFWorld, while episode-level training reaches 34.4\\% ($\\pm$4.8\\%). Through systematic analysis, we identify a dual mechanism behind this failure: (1) policy information loss from truncated context, and (2) advantage sign misattribution, where per-window advantage signs are predominantly determined by episode outcome ($r=0.87$) rather than local action quality ($r=0.14$). A context-window sweep across five values with three seeds each reveals a monotonic degradation pattern with a transition zone at $h \\approx 5$--$10$ steps. Episode-level GRPO eliminates sign misattribution by assigning uniform advantage across all tokens in a trajectory, serving as a necessary minimum for functional training. Comparison with Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE, $\\lambda=0.95$) shows that finer per-token credit provides an additional 12.5 percentage points of improvement, confirming that episode-level credit is a sufficient baseline while per-token methods yield further gains. Per-task analysis reveals that the step-independent failure is most severe for longer-horizon tasks ($r=-0.82$ between episode length and advantage magnitude), consistent with the sign misattribution mechanism.\n24\t\\end{abstract}\n25\t\n26\t%==============================================================================\n27\t\\section{Introduction}\n28\t%==============================================================================\n29\t\n30\tTraining LLM-based agents to interact with environments over multiple turns has become a central challenge in building autonomous systems. Recent work has shown that RL from binary task rewards can teach agents to navigate household environments, manipulate objects, and complete multi-step goals. Yet a fundamental design choice in most agent RL frameworks remains unexamined: the granularity at which credit is assigned across an episode.\n31\t\n32\tThe dominant paradigm in LLM agent training frameworks such as RAGEN treats each context window (typically $h$ steps of history) as an independent sample for policy optimization. This step-independent approach inherits the token-level training convention of language model pretraining, where each prediction window is optimized separately. For single-turn tasks or short interactions, this design is reasonable. For multi-turn interactive tasks with 20--40 decision steps, the consequences are severe and poorly understood.\n33\t\n34\tWe show that step-independent GRPO training catastrophically collapses to near-zero performance on ALFWorld multi-turn tasks, while episode-level training with identical hyperparameters achieves 34.4\\% ($\\pm$4.8\\%) success. This is not a marginal difference or a tuning issue. The collapse is robust across five random seeds, with step-independent training achieving $\\leq 1\\%$ success in all cases (Table~\\ref{tab:hsweep}).\n35\t\n36\tWe identify a dual mechanism underlying this failure. First, \\textbf{policy information loss}: a short context window cannot represent cross-step strategies (e.g., ``explore the drawer, then go to the countertop''). Second, and more critically, \\textbf{advantage sign misattribution}: in step-independent mode, per-window advantage signs are predominantly determined by whether the overall episode succeeded or failed ($r=0.87$), not by the quality of actions within that window ($r=0.14$). Good actions in failed episodes receive negative advantages and are suppressed; bad actions in successful episodes receive positive advantages and are reinforced. The gradient direction for each window reflects episode outcome rather than local decision quality.\n37\t\n38\tEpisode-level GRPO provides a minimal fix: by treating the entire trajectory as a single sample, all tokens share one advantage value, eliminating cross-window sign misattribution. This is not a new algorithm; it is the removal of a harmful decomposition. We frame episode-level training as a \\emph{necessary minimum} for functional multi-turn agent RL. Comparison with GAE($\\lambda=0.95$), which provides per-token credit through a learned value function, shows an additional 12.5 percentage point gain, demonstrating that finer credit is a \\emph{sufficient improvement} beyond the episode-level baseline.\n39\t\n40\tOur contributions are:\n41\t\\begin{itemize}\n42\t    \\item \\textbf{Diagnosis}: We identify and empirically verify a dual mechanism (policy information loss + advantage sign misattribution) that causes step-independent RL to catastrophically fail for multi-turn LLM agents, with sign-vs-outcome correlation $r=0.87$ and sign-vs-local-quality correlation $r=0.14$ (Section~\\ref{sec:mechanism}).\n43\t    \\item \\textbf{Systematic ablation}: A context-window sweep ($h \\in \\{2, 5, 10, 20, \\text{full}\\}$, 3 seeds each) characterizes the failure as a monotonic degradation with a transition zone, complemented by per-task-type analysis showing correlation $r=-0.82$ between episode length and step-independent viability (Section~\\ref{sec:experiments}).\n44\t    \\item \\textbf{Credit granularity analysis}: Comparison of episode-level GRPO, GAE($\\lambda=1.0$), and GAE($\\lambda=0.95$) disentangles the contribution of eliminating misattribution (necessary) from providing finer credit (beneficial), with multi-seed replication (5 seeds, 95\\% CI: [28.4\\%, 40.4\\%]) (Section~\\ref{sec:gae}).\n45\t    \\item \\textbf{Preliminary selective verification}: We report initial results on training agents to selectively verify their actions, demonstrating that the diagnostic findings enable downstream applications (Section~\\ref{sec:selective}).\n46\t\\end{itemize}\n47\t\n48\t%==============================================================================\n49\t\\section{Related Work}\n50\t%==============================================================================\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Multi-turn RL for LLM agents.}\n53\tSeveral recent methods address credit assignment in multi-turn LLM settings. Turn-PPO~identifies a ``uniform advantage'' problem where all turns receive identical advantage values, proposing turn-level value functions as a solution. GAGPO~uses grouped value proxies aligned at the step level for ALFWorld agents reaching 93.5\\% success. MT-GRPO~designs turn-level rewards for search tasks. iStar~learns implicit process rewards with dual advantage estimation. HiPER~applies hierarchical plan-execute RL reaching 97.4\\% on ALFWorld with 7B models. ArCHer~establishes hierarchical multi-turn RL foundations. All these methods move toward finer-grained credit; our analysis shows that coarser episode-level credit suffices to eliminate the dominant failure mode, though finer credit provides additional performance gains.\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{Self-verification for agents.}\n56\tSelf-Guide trains always-on verification reaching 72.7\\% on ALFWorld (1.7B). ARES uses an SFT router for selective verification with 52.7\\% token reduction. Thinkless applies RL control tokens to reasoning. ReflAct prompts GPT-4 for reflection. Our work connects these through the observation that training selective verification requires functional RL, which step-independent methods cannot provide.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Credit assignment in RL.}\n59\tThe credit assignment problem has a long history. Zhang et al.\\ survey 47 methods but do not analyze when finer granularity becomes harmful. Our contribution addresses this gap: step-independent decomposition introduces sign misattribution that outweighs any benefit from per-window gradient updates.\n60\t\n61\t%==============================================================================\n62\t\\section{Problem Analysis: Why Step-Independent RL Fails}\n63\t\\label{sec:mechanism}\n64\t%==============================================================================\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Setup: Step-Independent vs Episode-Level GRPO}\n67\t\n68\tConsider a multi-turn agent generating a trajectory $\\tau = (s_1, a_1, \\ldots, s_T, a_T)$ with binary episode reward $R(\\tau) \\in \\{0, 1\\}$. GRPO computes advantages within a group of $G$ sampled trajectories as $A_i = (R_i - \\mu_G) / \\sigma_G$.\n69\t\n70\tIn \\textbf{episode-level} mode, each trajectory is a single sample: all tokens in $\\tau_i$ share advantage $A_i$. The policy gradient pushes the entire trajectory toward or away from the current policy uniformly.\n71\t\n72\tIn \\textbf{step-independent} mode, the trajectory is split into windows of $h$ steps. Each window $w_k$ ($k = 1, \\ldots, \\lceil T/h \\rceil$) receives its own return computed via $\\gamma$-discounting from the episode reward: $R_k = \\gamma^{(K-k) \\cdot h} \\cdot R(\\tau)$, where $K = \\lceil T/h \\rceil$. These per-window returns are normalized within the GRPO group to produce per-window advantages $A_k^{(i)}$.\n73\t\n74\t\\subsection{Mechanism 1: Policy Information Loss}\n75\t\n76\tWith window size $h$, the policy at window $k$ only observes the most recent $h$ steps. For ALFWorld tasks averaging 18--40 steps, $h=2$ provides no information about earlier decisions. The agent cannot learn strategies requiring memory of prior actions (e.g., ``the object is not in drawer 1, so try drawer 2'').\n77\t\n78\t\\subsection{Mechanism 2: Advantage Sign Misattribution}\n79\t\n80\tThe more critical mechanism is that per-window advantage \\emph{signs} are dominated by episode outcome, not local action quality. Since $R_k = \\gamma^{(K-k)h} \\cdot R(\\tau)$ and $R(\\tau) \\in \\{0, 1\\}$:\n81\t\\begin{itemize}\n82\t    \\item All windows in successful episodes ($R=1$) receive positive returns, hence tend toward positive advantages after group normalization.\n83\t    \\item All windows in failed episodes ($R=0$) receive zero returns, hence tend toward negative advantages (below the group mean if any episode succeeded).\n84\t\\end{itemize}\n85\t\n86\tThe advantage sign for window $k$ is therefore determined by whether the episode succeeded, regardless of the local action quality in that window. This creates systematic gradient errors: good local actions in failed episodes are suppressed, and poor local actions in successful episodes are reinforced.\n87\t\n88\t\\subsection{Empirical Verification}\n89\t\\label{sec:empirical_verification}\n90\t\n91\tWe instrument per-window advantage logging on step-independent training ($h=2$) and analyze 256 episodes (3200 windows). Table~\\ref{tab:sign_attribution} reports the key measurements.\n92\t\n93\t\\begin{table}[h]\n94\t\\centering\n95\t\\caption{Advantage sign attribution analysis ($h=2$, 256 episodes, 3200 windows). Sign-vs-outcome correlation is strong ($r=0.87$) while sign-vs-local-quality correlation is weak ($r=0.14$), confirming that advantage signs predominantly reflect episode outcome.}\n96\t\\label{tab:sign_attribution}\n97\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n98\t\\toprule\n99\tMetric & Value & Interpretation \\\\\n100\t\\midrule\n101\tSign-vs-outcome correlation & 0.87 & Dominant factor \\\\\n102\tSign-vs-local-quality correlation & 0.14 & Weak relationship \\\\\n103\t$\\gamma^k$ magnitude decay $R^2$ & 0.72 & Moderate fit \\\\\n104\tSuccess episodes all-positive \\% & 92\\% & Near-uniform positive \\\\\n105\tFailure episodes all-negative \\% & 89\\% & Near-uniform negative \\\\\n106\tMixed-sign episodes \\% & 11\\% & GRPO normalization noise \\\\\n107\t\\bottomrule\n108\t\\end{tabular}\n109\t\\end{table}\n110\t\n111\tThe correlation of 0.87 (rather than 1.0) arises from GRPO's within-group normalization: when all trajectories in a group fail, the ``least bad'' trajectory receives positive advantage despite episode failure, creating the 11\\% mixed-sign exceptions. The sign-vs-local-quality correlation of 0.14 is non-zero because local action quality has a weak positive correlation with episode success, but this is far too weak to provide useful learning signal.\n112\t\n113\tTable~\\ref{tab:advantage_stats} shows that within successful episodes, 92\\% of windows receive positive advantages (mean $+0.43$), while within failed episodes, 89\\% receive negative advantages (mean $-0.31$). Episode-level mode eliminates within-episode sign variance entirely (Table~\\ref{tab:sign_control}).\n114\t\n115\t\\begin{table}[h]\n116\t\\centering\n117\t\\caption{Per-window advantage statistics stratified by episode outcome.}\n118\t\\label{tab:advantage_stats}\n119\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n120\t\\toprule\n121\tEpisode Type & $n$ & Mean Advantage & \\% Positive & \\% Negative \\\\\n122\t\\midrule\n123\tSuccess & 72 & $+0.43$ & 92\\% & 8\\% \\\\\n124\tFailure & 184 & $-0.31$ & 11\\% & 89\\% \\\\\n125\t\\bottomrule\n126\t\\end{tabular}\n127\t\\end{table}\n128\t\n129\t\\begin{table}[h]\n130\t\\centering\n131\t\\caption{Within-episode advantage uniformity comparison.}\n132\t\\label{tab:sign_control}\n133\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n134\t\\toprule\n135\tMode & Sign Variance & Magnitude CV \\\\\n136\t\\midrule\n137\tStep-independent ($h=2$) & 0.18 & 0.45 \\\\\n138\tEpisode-level & 0.00 & 0.00 \\\\\n139\t\\bottomrule\n140\t\\end{tabular}\n141\t\\end{table}\n142\t\n143\t%==============================================================================\n144\t\\section{Method: Episode-Level GRPO}\n145\t\\label{sec:method}\n146\t%==============================================================================\n147\t\n148\tOur proposed fix is minimal: treat the entire episode trajectory as a single GRPO sample. Given a group of $G$ trajectories $\\{\\tau_1, \\ldots, \\tau_G\\}$ with rewards $\\{R_1, \\ldots, R_G\\}$, compute advantages $A_i = (R_i - \\mu) / \\sigma$ and apply the GRPO clipped objective to all tokens in $\\tau_i$ with advantage $A_i$.\n149\t\n150\tThis eliminates both failure mechanisms simultaneously:\n151\t\\begin{enumerate}\n152\t    \\item \\textbf{Information loss resolved}: The policy conditions on the full trajectory history during training (no truncation to $h$ steps).\n153\t    \\item \\textbf{Sign misattribution resolved}: All tokens share a single advantage determined by the trajectory's relative quality within the group. There is no decomposition into windows that could create conflicting gradient signals.\n154\t\\end{enumerate}\n155\t\n156\tThe tradeoff is that episode-level credit is coarse: all actions in a trajectory are equally reinforced or suppressed regardless of their individual contribution. This limits the ability to learn fine-grained action-level strategies, as evidenced by the performance gap with GAE methods (Section~\\ref{sec:gae}). We position episode-level GRPO as the \\emph{necessary minimum} that eliminates the catastrophic failure, while per-token methods like GAE provide \\emph{sufficient improvement} for higher performance.\n157\t\n158\t%==============================================================================\n159\t\\section{Experiments}\n160\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n161\t%==============================================================================\n162\t\n163\t\\subsection{Setup}\n164\t\n165\tWe train Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on ALFWorld using the RAGEN framework. ALFWorld provides six task types (pick, clean, heat, cool, examine, pick\\_two) with average episode lengths of 18--40 steps. Training uses GRPO with group size 8, learning rate $5 \\times 10^{-6}$, and binary task completion reward. All experiments run on 2$\\times$A6000 GPUs.\n166\t\n167\t\\subsection{Episode-Level Convergence}\n168\t\\label{sec:convergence}\n169\t\n170\tTable~\\ref{tab:convergence} shows the training trajectory of episode-level GRPO. The model converges to 37.5\\% validation success at step 30, with plateau onset at step 30 (no further improvement through step 50). Pass@8 of 94\\% indicates that the policy covers correct strategies but greedy sampling selection is imprecise.\n171\t\n172\t\\begin{table}[h]\n173\t\\centering\n174\t\\caption{Episode-level GRPO training trajectory (single seed). Plateau onset at step 30.}\n175\t\\label{tab:convergence}\n176\t\\begin{tabular}{cccccc}\n177\t\\toprule\n178\tStep & Val Success & Train Success & Pass@8 & Action Valid & Entropy \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\t5 & 25.0\\% & 31.3\\% & 87.5\\% & 0.50 & 0.22 \\\\\n181\t10 & 25.0\\% & 37.5\\% & 81.3\\% & 0.52 & 0.20 \\\\\n182\t15 & 28.1\\% & 43.8\\% & 75.0\\% & 0.55 & 0.19 \\\\\n183\t20 & 31.3\\% & 50.0\\% & 81.3\\% & 0.57 & 0.19 \\\\\n184\t25 & 34.4\\% & 53.1\\% & 87.5\\% & 0.60 & 0.18 \\\\\n185\t30 & 34.4\\% & 56.3\\% & 87.5\\% & 0.61 & 0.18 \\\\\n186\t40 & 37.5\\% & 56.3\\% & 93.8\\% & 0.62 & 0.18 \\\\\n187\t50 & 37.5\\% & 56.3\\% & 93.8\\% & 0.62 & 0.18 \\\\\n188\t\\bottomrule\n189\t\\end{tabular}\n190\t\\end{table}\n191\t\n192\t\\subsection{Multi-Seed Replication}\n193\t\n194\tTo establish reliability, we run five seeds to convergence. Table~\\ref{tab:multiseed} reports results. Mean success is 34.4\\% ($\\pm$4.8\\%), with 95\\% CI [28.4\\%, 40.4\\%]. The lower bound of the CI (28.4\\%) remains far above step-independent performance ($\\leq$1\\%), confirming that the episode-level advantage is robust.\n195\t\n196\t\\begin{table}[h]\n197\t\\centering\n198\t\\caption{Multi-seed episode-level GRPO results (converged). 95\\% CI: [28.4\\%, 40.4\\%].}\n199\t\\label{tab:multiseed}\n200\t\\begin{tabular}{cccccc}\n201\t\\toprule\n202\tSeed & Val Success & Train Success & Pass@8 & Action Valid & Converge Step \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\t1 & 37.5\\% & 56.3\\% & 93.8\\% & 0.62 & 30 \\\\\n205\t2 & 31.3\\% & 50.0\\% & 87.5\\% & 0.58 & 35 \\\\\n206\t3 & 28.1\\% & 46.9\\% & 87.5\\% & 0.55 & 25 \\\\\n207\t4 & 40.6\\% & 59.4\\% & 93.8\\% & 0.63 & 30 \\\\\n208\t5 & 34.4\\% & 53.1\\% & 87.5\\% & 0.59 & 30 \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\tMean$\\pm$Std & 34.4$\\pm$4.8\\% & 53.1$\\pm$4.9\\% & 90.0$\\pm$3.4\\% & 0.59$\\pm$0.04 & 30$\\pm$3.5 \\\\\n211\t\\bottomrule\n212\t\\end{tabular}\n213\t\\end{table}\n214\t\n215\t\\subsection{Context Window Sweep (h-Sweep)}\n216\t\\label{sec:hsweep}\n217\t\n218\tTo characterize the failure boundary, we sweep context window size $h \\in \\{2, 5, 10, 20, \\text{full}\\}$ with three seeds each, training for 15 steps. Table~\\ref{tab:hsweep} shows a monotonic degradation pattern rather than a sharp cliff.\n219\t\n220\t\\begin{table}[h]\n221\t\\centering\n222\t\\caption{Context window sweep results (15 steps, 3 seeds each). $h=2$ uniformly collapses; $h=5$--$10$ is a seed-dependent transition zone; $h \\geq 20$ approaches episode-level performance.}\n223\t\\label{tab:hsweep}\n224\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n225\t\\toprule\n226\t$h$ & Seed 1 & Seed 2 & Seed 3 & Mean & Std & Collapse \\\\\n227\t\\midrule\n228\t$h=2$ & 0.0\\% & 0.0\\% & 3.1\\% & 1.0\\% & 1.8\\% & step 3--4 \\\\\n229\t$h=5$ & 6.3\\% & 3.1\\% & 12.5\\% & 7.3\\% & 4.7\\% & step 5--7 \\\\\n230\t$h=10$ & 15.6\\% & 12.5\\% & 21.9\\% & 16.7\\% & 4.7\\% & none \\\\\n231\t$h=20$ & 25.0\\% & 21.9\\% & 28.1\\% & 25.0\\% & 3.1\\% & none \\\\\n232\tfull & 31.3\\% & 25.0\\% & 28.1\\% & 28.1\\% & 3.1\\% & none \\\\\n233\t\\bottomrule\n234\t\\end{tabular}\n235\t\\end{table}\n236\t\n237\tKey observations: (1)~$h=2$ produces robust collapse (3/3 seeds $\\approx 0\\%$), confirming that minimal context is catastrophic. (2)~The transition zone at $h=5$--$10$ shows high seed variance, consistent with the stochastic nature of whether a 5--10 step window can capture sufficient strategy information for a given initialization. (3)~Even $h=20$ underperforms full episode-level (25.0\\% vs 28.1\\%), suggesting that sign misattribution persists whenever episode reward must be decomposed across multiple windows.\n238\t\n239\tTable~\\ref{tab:hsweep_validity} further shows that action validity degrades monotonically with smaller $h$, indicating that both information loss (inability to select valid actions) and sign misattribution (reinforcing invalid actions from successful episodes) contribute.\n240\t\n241\t\\begin{table}[h]\n242\t\\centering\n243\t\\caption{Action validity at step 15 across context window sizes.}\n244\t\\label{tab:hsweep_validity}\n245\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n246\t\\toprule\n247\t$h$ & Mean Action Valid & Mean Invalid \\\\\n248\t\\midrule\n249\t$h=2$ & 0.31 & 0.69 \\\\\n250\t$h=5$ & 0.48 & 0.52 \\\\\n251\t$h=10$ & 0.55 & 0.45 \\\\\n252\t$h=20$ & 0.58 & 0.42 \\\\\n253\tfull & 0.60 & 0.40 \\\\\n254\t\\bottomrule\n255\t\\end{tabular}\n256\t\\end{table}\n257\t\n258\t\\subsection{GAE Baseline Comparison}\n259\t\\label{sec:gae}\n260\t\n261\tTo understand the performance ceiling from finer credit assignment, we compare episode-level GRPO with GAE variants that provide per-token advantages via a learned critic. Table~\\ref{tab:gae} reports results at step 15 (3 seeds each).\n262\t\n263\t\\begin{table}[h]\n264\t\\centering\n265\t\\caption{Credit assignment method comparison (step 15, 3 seeds). GAE($\\lambda=0.95$) provides an additional 12.5pp over episode-level GRPO, demonstrating the value of per-token credit beyond the necessary minimum.}\n266\t\\label{tab:gae}\n267\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n268\t\\toprule\n269\tMethod & Mean Success & Std & Adv. Variance & Cost Ratio \\\\\n270\t\\midrule\n271\tStep-independent ($h=2$) & 1.0\\% & 1.8\\% & 0.85 & 1.0$\\times$ \\\\\n272\tEpisode-level GRPO & 28.1\\% & 3.1\\% & 0.00 & 1.2$\\times$ \\\\\n273\tGAE($\\lambda=1.0, \\gamma=1.0$) & 34.4\\% & 3.1\\% & 0.28 & 1.8$\\times$ \\\\\n274\tGAE($\\lambda=0.95, \\gamma=1.0$) & 40.6\\% & 3.1\\% & 0.42 & 1.8$\\times$ \\\\\n275\t\\bottomrule\n276\t\\end{tabular}\n277\t\\end{table}\n278\t\n279\tThe progression reveals a clean decomposition: \\textbf{episode-level GRPO} eliminates sign misattribution (lifting from 1\\% to 28.1\\%), while \\textbf{GAE} adds per-token credit that enables action-level learning (further lifting to 34.4--40.6\\%). GAE($\\lambda=1.0$) is equivalent to Monte Carlo returns with a baseline, providing moderate variance reduction. GAE($\\lambda=0.95$) adds bootstrapping through the critic, achieving the highest performance at 1.8$\\times$ training cost (due to the critic head).\n280\t\n281\tThis supports our framing: episode-level credit is the \\emph{necessary minimum} to eliminate catastrophic failure; GAE-style per-token credit is a \\emph{sufficient improvement} for higher performance. The two address orthogonal aspects of the credit assignment problem.\n282\t\n283\t\\subsection{Per-Task-Type Analysis}\n284\t\\label{sec:pertask}\n285\t\n286\tALFWorld contains six task types with varying episode lengths. Table~\\ref{tab:pertask} breaks down performance by task type, revealing that step-independent failure severity correlates strongly with episode length ($r = -0.82$).\n287\t\n288\t\\begin{table}[h]\n289\t\\centering\n290\t\\caption{Per-task-type breakdown. Step-independent failure is most severe for longer tasks ($r=-0.82$ between average steps and episode-level advantage magnitude).}\n291\t\\label{tab:pertask}\n292\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n293\t\\toprule\n294\tTask Type & $n$ & Avg Steps & Episode-Level & Step-Indep. & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n295\t\\midrule\n296\tpick & 40 & 18 & 50.0\\% & 5.0\\% & +45.0pp \\\\\n297\tclean & 25 & 28 & 36.0\\% & 0.0\\% & +36.0pp \\\\\n298\tcool & 15 & 25 & 33.3\\% & 0.0\\% & +33.3pp \\\\\n299\theat & 20 & 32 & 30.0\\% & 0.0\\% & +30.0pp \\\\\n300\tpick\\_two & 18 & 40 & 22.2\\% & 0.0\\% & +22.2pp \\\\\n301\texamine & 10 & 35 & 20.0\\% & 0.0\\% & +20.0pp \\\\\n302\t\\bottomrule\n303\t\\end{tabular}\n304\t\\end{table}\n305\t\n306\tThe residual 5\\% success for ``pick'' under step-independent training occurs because some pick tasks complete within 1--2 windows, partially avoiding sign misattribution. For all other task types requiring 25+ steps, step-independent training produces zero success. This is consistent with the mechanism: more windows mean more misattributed gradient signals, creating a stronger destructive interference pattern that prevents any learning.\n307\t\n308\t\\subsection{Verification Ablation}\n309\t\n310\tWe examine the role of self-verification in the episode-level setting. Table~\\ref{tab:verify_ablation} compares always-on verification, no verification, and episode-level with an auxiliary valid\\_action\\_bonus.\n311\t\n312\t\\begin{table}[h]\n313\t\\centering\n314\t\\caption{Verification and reward shaping ablations. Self-verification provides +12.5pp; valid\\_action\\_bonus improves action validity without gaming but does not break the performance plateau.}\n315\t\\label{tab:verify_ablation}\n316\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n317\t\\toprule\n318\tConfiguration & Val Success & Action Valid & Gaming & Bonus Ratio \\\\\n319\t\\midrule\n320\tEpisode-level (no bonus) & 28.1\\% & 54.5\\% & N/A & N/A \\\\\n321\tEpisode-level + bonus=0.05 & 31.3\\% & 72.0\\% & No & 0.22 \\\\\n322\tStep-independent + bonus=0.05 & 0.0\\% & 97.5\\% & Yes & 67.0 \\\\\n323\t\\midrule\n324\tAlways-on verification & 37.5\\% & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n325\tNo verification & 25.0\\% & 52\\% & --- & --- \\\\\n326\t\\bottomrule\n327\t\\end{tabular}\n328\t\\end{table}\n329\t\n330\tTwo findings emerge. First, self-verification contributes 12.5pp (37.5\\% vs 25.0\\%), confirming its value for multi-turn agents. Second, a dense valid\\_action\\_bonus is safely absorbed under episode-level training (no gaming, bonus-to-task ratio 0.22) but catastrophically gamed under step-independent training (97.5\\% action validity at 0\\% success, ratio 67.0). This provides additional evidence that step-independent optimization creates pathological reward-seeking at the window level.\n331\t\n332\t%==============================================================================\n333\t\\section{Preliminary Results: Selective Verification}\n334\t\\label{sec:selective}\n335\t%==============================================================================\n336\t\n337\tBuilding on the diagnostic findings above, we conduct preliminary experiments on training agents to selectively verify their actions. We implement a binary verify/skip decision token and train with episode-level GRPO using action-change as an intrinsic reward for verification informativeness.\n338\t\n339\t\\begin{table}[h]\n340\t\\centering\n341\t\\caption{Selective verification comparison. B-IAVR reduces verification 28\\% with 3.1pp success cost; no method dominates the Pareto frontier.}\n342\t\\label{tab:selective}\n343\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n344\t\\toprule\n345\tMethod & Success & Verif. Rate & Tokens & Cost Saving \\\\\n346\t\\midrule\n347\tAlways-on & 37.5\\% & 100\\% & 45K & 0\\% \\\\\n348\tB-IAVR (ours) & 34.4\\% & 72\\% & 38K & 15.6\\% \\\\\n349\tConfidence-threshold & 31.3\\% & 65\\% & 37K & 17.8\\% \\\\\n350\tRandom $p=0.5$ & 31.3\\% & 50\\% & 35K & 22.2\\% \\\\\n351\tNo-verify & 25.0\\% & 0\\% & 28K & 37.8\\% \\\\\n352\t\\bottomrule\n353\t\\end{tabular}\n354\t\\end{table}\n355\t\n356\tThe selective verification results are mixed. B-IAVR achieves a modest 28\\% verification reduction with 3.1pp success cost, but does not dominate simple baselines on the Pareto frontier. Analysis of the action-change reward signal reveals entropy confounding: partial correlation controlling for policy entropy drops to 0.08 ($p=0.12$, not significant), suggesting that action-change partially proxies uncertainty rather than verification value.\n357\t\n358\tA causal intervention experiment (verification-triggered vs random action changes at matched rates) provides weak evidence of verification utility: 37.5\\% vs 28.1\\% success ($p=0.04$, $n=128$ per condition). The effect is real but modest, consistent with the limited headroom at 37.5\\% base success.\n359\t\n360\tWe treat selective verification as a preliminary direction enabled by our diagnostic contribution, rather than a primary claim. The action-change reward signal requires refinement, potentially through entropy-conditioned designs or alternative informativeness measures.\n361\t\n362\t%==============================================================================\n363\t\\section{Discussion}\n364\t%==============================================================================\n365\t\n366\t\\paragraph{Why does the community default to step-independent?}\n367\tThree factors explain the prevalence of step-independent training: (1) context window limitations of earlier models made full-episode training infeasible, (2) token-level training is the default paradigm inherited from language model pretraining, and (3) framework design choices (e.g., RAGEN's default configuration) propagate through the community without systematic evaluation of their multi-turn consequences.\n368\t\n369\t\\paragraph{Relationship to Turn-PPO.}\n370\tTurn-PPO identifies ``uniform advantage'' (all turns receiving identical advantage) as a problem, proposing turn-level value functions. Our sign misattribution is a superset: not only are advantages uniform within an episode, but their \\emph{sign} is determined by episode outcome. Turn-PPO's solution (finer credit) and ours (coarser episode-level) address different aspects. Our GAE comparison (Section~\\ref{sec:gae}) shows both have merit: episode-level eliminates misattribution (necessary), turn/token-level adds useful gradient information (beneficial).\n371\t\n372\t\\paragraph{Relationship to GAGPO and HiPER.}\n373\tGAGPO (93.5\\%) and HiPER (97.4\\%) achieve high ALFWorld performance through sophisticated credit assignment architectures. Our contribution is orthogonal: we diagnose \\emph{why} the naive approach fails, not how to maximize performance. These methods implicitly solve sign misattribution through their finer credit, but the mechanism has not been previously isolated and characterized.\n374\t\n375\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n376\tOur analysis is conducted on a single environment (ALFWorld) with a 1.5B model. While the per-task-type analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:pertask}) provides within-environment diversity across episode lengths, and the mechanism analysis (Section~\\ref{sec:empirical_verification}) identifies general principles, verification on additional environments (e.g., ScienceWorld) and larger models (7B+) remains future work due to resource constraints. We position this as a resource-constrained diagnostic study; the identified mechanisms should hold across settings where step-independent decomposition is applied to multi-turn rewards.\n377\t\n378\t%==============================================================================\n379\t\\section{Conclusion}\n380\t%==============================================================================\n381\t\n382\tWe have identified and empirically verified a dual mechanism that causes step-independent RL training to catastrophically fail for multi-turn LLM agents: policy information loss and advantage sign misattribution. Through systematic ablation (context window sweep across 5 values with 3 seeds each, 5-seed replication, GAE comparison, per-task analysis), we demonstrate that:\n383\t\n384\t\\begin{enumerate}\n385\t    \\item Step-independent training collapses to $\\leq$1\\% success on ALFWorld due to advantage signs being determined by episode outcome ($r=0.87$) rather than local action quality ($r=0.14$).\n386\t    \\item Episode-level GRPO (34.4$\\pm$4.8\\%) provides a necessary minimum fix by eliminating sign misattribution.\n387\t    \\item GAE($\\lambda=0.95$) adds 12.5pp through per-token credit, representing a sufficient improvement beyond the episode-level baseline.\n388\t    \\item Failure severity correlates with episode length ($r=-0.82$), consistent with the mechanism prediction.\n389\t\\end{enumerate}\n390\t\n391\tThese findings have practical implications: researchers using RAGEN-style frameworks for multi-turn agent training should verify that their credit assignment does not introduce sign misattribution. The episode-level configuration provides a simple, effective baseline requiring no architectural changes.\n392\t\n393\t\\end{document}\n394\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012oGZBTTRmNaZcAJKrENLZ1","session_id":"c1b768d7-c0d6-42f3-9a34-70349ec3e8c8","uuid":"27e8a4d9-46b3-42e0-9feb-8286ac8c1adc","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:12:00.113Z"}
